WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.980
I want you to think, just for a second, about exactly how you got your current job.

00:05.280 --> 00:06.960
Oh, that's a, yeah, that's a big question.

00:07.040 --> 00:07.160
Right.

00:07.440 --> 00:11.560
Like, if you really sit down and trace the breadcrumbs back to the very beginning,

00:12.120 --> 00:16.180
what was the actual mechanism that got you into that room and sitting in that chair?

00:16.560 --> 00:18.620
Most people don't want to think about that too closely.

00:18.880 --> 00:19.740
Oh, they don't.

00:20.060 --> 00:23.980
Because, I mean, was it just your raw, undeniable skill,

00:24.160 --> 00:27.960
shining like a beacon, blinding the hiring manager with your sheer competence?

00:27.960 --> 00:29.460
We all want to believe that.

00:30.000 --> 00:32.600
We desperately prefer the beacon narrative, you know?

00:32.680 --> 00:33.480
But we really do.

00:34.120 --> 00:37.820
Or, if we are being completely honest with ourselves,

00:38.180 --> 00:40.180
was it a degree you had framed on a wall?

00:40.500 --> 00:43.060
Was it a glowing reference from someone who, you know,

00:43.080 --> 00:44.860
just happened to know someone already in the building?

00:45.000 --> 00:48.080
Or geographic luck, right, just where you happen to grow up.

00:48.220 --> 00:48.640
Exactly.

00:49.240 --> 00:52.920
We are almost conditioned to believe that when we apply for a job,

00:53.260 --> 00:56.240
or like a mortgage, or a university program,

00:56.240 --> 01:01.840
the person sitting on the other side of the desk has this magical x-ray vision.

01:01.960 --> 01:02.940
Right, the x-ray machine.

01:03.180 --> 01:04.960
Yeah, we want to believe they can just look at us,

01:05.340 --> 01:06.560
pierce right through the resume,

01:07.080 --> 01:08.880
see our pure underlying competence,

01:09.100 --> 01:11.260
and reward it based purely on merit.

01:11.260 --> 01:14.540
But today, we are going to explore a really compelling,

01:14.860 --> 01:17.180
deeply analytical new argument that says,

01:17.540 --> 01:20.320
basically, this x-ray machine does not exist.

01:20.420 --> 01:21.120
It's a total myth.

01:21.300 --> 01:21.560
Yeah.

01:21.960 --> 01:26.460
And in fact, evaluating true human competence is actually incredibly dangerous.

01:27.040 --> 01:28.400
And to manage that danger,

01:28.620 --> 01:32.360
society has built these massive, remarkably expensive,

01:33.080 --> 01:35.620
and highly exclusionary workarounds.

01:35.620 --> 01:41.320
It's just a profound shift in how we understand the basic machinery of opportunity today.

01:41.640 --> 01:42.460
And to pull this apart,

01:43.020 --> 01:45.640
we are drawing on a phenomenal source for this deep dive.

01:45.780 --> 01:46.880
It really is a great source.

01:47.000 --> 01:49.720
It's a comprehensive essay published in July of 2026

01:49.720 --> 01:52.480
by an independent researcher writing under the name Flyxion.

01:52.780 --> 01:52.980
Right.

01:53.340 --> 01:55.340
The essay is called Developmental Opacity,

01:55.720 --> 01:57.000
and it was written as a direct,

01:57.400 --> 02:00.620
highly rigorous response to this incredibly influential book

02:00.620 --> 02:03.460
called Inheritocracy by Eliza Filby.

02:03.460 --> 02:04.700
Okay, so let's unpack this,

02:04.780 --> 02:06.460
because our mission for this deep dive

02:06.460 --> 02:10.360
is to figure out exactly how wealth translates into opportunity today.

02:10.560 --> 02:13.160
And we aren't just talking about trust funds here.

02:13.380 --> 02:17.080
No, no, not just passing down stock portfolios or bags of cash.

02:17.380 --> 02:20.100
We are investigating the invisible machinery

02:20.100 --> 02:26.340
of how your potential, like, your actual human capability is judged.

02:26.440 --> 02:29.520
And why that judgment has become so inextricably tied up

02:29.520 --> 02:31.440
in the resources your parents just happen to have.

02:31.540 --> 02:31.980
Exactly.

02:31.980 --> 02:35.340
But before we get to the author's fascinating new theory

02:35.340 --> 02:36.800
about how competence is measured,

02:36.940 --> 02:39.400
which is the developmental opacity part,

02:39.940 --> 02:42.740
we really need to lay out the problem it's responding to.

02:42.860 --> 02:44.400
Right. Setting the stage is crucial.

02:44.700 --> 02:46.820
We have to look at the generational wealth gap first.

02:46.940 --> 02:50.620
Yeah, because to understand the proposed mechanism

02:50.620 --> 02:52.400
of how inequality functions,

02:53.180 --> 02:55.020
you first have to understand the diagnosis

02:55.020 --> 02:58.340
that Eliza Filby lays out in Inheritocracy.

02:58.540 --> 02:59.900
So what's her baseline argument there?

02:59.900 --> 03:02.500
Well, grounded in extensive economic data,

03:02.700 --> 03:04.680
her baseline is that opportunity in Britain,

03:05.120 --> 03:07.880
and really, by extension, the broader Anglophone world,

03:08.000 --> 03:10.460
like the U.S., Canada, Australia opportunity,

03:10.780 --> 03:14.060
is increasingly conditioned almost entirely on parental wealth.

03:14.160 --> 03:15.860
Which is a massive paradigm shift.

03:16.120 --> 03:16.820
It's huge.

03:17.180 --> 03:20.060
Opportunity is no longer primarily conditioned on wages,

03:20.060 --> 03:23.280
and it's not even conditioned on educational attainment

03:23.280 --> 03:25.180
in the way it used to be during, you know,

03:25.280 --> 03:26.840
the latter half of the 20th century.

03:27.500 --> 03:32.360
And the reasons she gives for this shift are incredibly stark.

03:32.500 --> 03:34.520
I mean, frankly, they're a bit terrifying

03:34.520 --> 03:38.120
if you are a young person entering the workforce right now.

03:38.400 --> 03:40.340
The basic math of living has broken down.

03:40.500 --> 03:41.560
Completely broken down.

03:41.700 --> 03:41.800
Yeah.

03:41.940 --> 03:43.840
Wages have just stagnated relative

03:43.840 --> 03:46.360
to the explosive runaway costs of living,

03:46.800 --> 03:47.780
specifically housing.

03:48.040 --> 03:49.000
Housing is a big one, yeah.

03:49.000 --> 03:50.320
And at the exact same time,

03:50.400 --> 03:53.340
the historical return on education is weakening.

03:53.820 --> 03:55.100
Like, having a bachelor's degree

03:55.100 --> 03:57.960
simply doesn't guarantee that middle-class stability anymore.

03:58.020 --> 03:59.760
No, the house, the two cars,

04:00.060 --> 04:02.980
that whole promise from 20 or 30 years ago is gone.

04:03.180 --> 04:04.060
Especially now,

04:04.360 --> 04:06.680
with the intense, accelerating pressure

04:06.680 --> 04:08.380
from automation and AI

04:08.380 --> 04:11.120
really squeezing the white-collar labor market.

04:11.240 --> 04:12.980
That is the major friction point.

04:13.240 --> 04:14.540
And Filby locates the root

04:14.540 --> 04:16.280
of this current suffocating dynamic

04:16.280 --> 04:18.880
in a very specific historical conjunction

04:18.880 --> 04:21.780
that enormously benefited the baby boomer generation.

04:22.340 --> 04:23.160
Now, we should be clear here,

04:23.420 --> 04:25.300
she is talking about a Western cohort,

04:25.560 --> 04:26.940
not a global one.

04:27.240 --> 04:29.340
Right, a specific demographic in the West.

04:29.960 --> 04:30.780
But for them,

04:31.200 --> 04:34.060
they experience a genuinely unrepeatable alignment

04:34.060 --> 04:35.840
of macroeconomic forces.

04:36.060 --> 04:37.900
Unrepeatable is definitely the key word there.

04:38.320 --> 04:39.800
Let's look at the timeline they wrote,

04:40.100 --> 04:41.080
because it's wild.

04:41.360 --> 04:42.100
It really is.

04:42.100 --> 04:45.820
They had widening, subsidized access to higher education

04:45.820 --> 04:47.580
at exactly the right time,

04:47.880 --> 04:50.420
when a degree actually made you stand out.

04:50.660 --> 04:52.300
And then they entered professional fields

04:52.300 --> 04:53.900
and locked in career trajectories

04:53.900 --> 04:55.780
long before those fields were exposed

04:55.780 --> 04:59.020
to intense wage-crushing pressures

04:59.020 --> 05:00.460
of globalized competition.

05:00.600 --> 05:01.520
Or outsourcing, yeah.

05:01.640 --> 05:03.240
And then the absolute kicker,

05:03.440 --> 05:05.180
in the 1980s and 90s,

05:05.520 --> 05:08.160
they rode this massive, unprecedented asset

05:08.160 --> 05:09.120
and housing boom.

05:09.120 --> 05:11.540
Which wildly inflated the value of the property

05:11.540 --> 05:13.120
and the pensions they had already managed

05:13.120 --> 05:14.080
to acquire for cheap.

05:14.100 --> 05:15.940
And this is where Filby is quite precise, right?

05:16.160 --> 05:17.580
She avoids the trap

05:17.580 --> 05:20.100
of simple generational warfare.

05:20.260 --> 05:21.480
Yeah, she points out that

05:21.480 --> 05:23.320
none of this massive wealth accumulation

05:23.320 --> 05:25.360
was the result of individual merit

05:25.360 --> 05:27.560
in any straightforward moral sense.

05:27.980 --> 05:29.460
Like, they didn't magically work

05:29.460 --> 05:31.320
10 times harder than the generation

05:31.320 --> 05:32.680
before them or after them.

05:32.880 --> 05:33.340
Exactly.

05:33.680 --> 05:35.320
It was, as she puts it,

05:35.400 --> 05:37.420
simply a moment in time.

05:37.520 --> 05:38.440
A moment in time.

05:38.440 --> 05:40.200
So the millennial and Gen Z experience

05:40.200 --> 05:41.340
isn't just different

05:41.340 --> 05:44.040
because that specific economic boom

05:44.040 --> 05:44.980
failed to repeat.

05:45.800 --> 05:47.100
It is fundamentally different

05:47.100 --> 05:49.780
because the entire generational cost structure

05:49.780 --> 05:50.640
has inverted.

05:50.960 --> 05:52.460
Oh, the cost structure inversion.

05:52.740 --> 05:53.980
Here's where it gets really interesting.

05:54.120 --> 05:55.260
Because when you lay out

05:55.260 --> 05:56.720
what costs what

05:56.720 --> 05:57.960
for different generations,

05:58.280 --> 05:59.960
it paints such a vivid,

06:00.160 --> 06:01.440
almost absurd picture.

06:01.700 --> 06:03.180
Think about the daily reality.

06:03.560 --> 06:04.740
Yeah, for the baby boomers.

06:04.820 --> 06:04.960
Yeah.

06:04.960 --> 06:05.900
Relatively speaking,

06:06.020 --> 06:07.420
going out and buying a color TV

06:07.420 --> 06:08.720
or booking a flight

06:08.720 --> 06:10.120
was a massive luxury.

06:10.320 --> 06:12.800
University was a significant expense too, yes.

06:12.920 --> 06:15.080
But the truly foundational pillars

06:15.080 --> 06:16.740
of adult life,

06:16.800 --> 06:18.440
like buying a starter home,

06:18.600 --> 06:20.140
paying for basic health care,

06:20.260 --> 06:21.520
raising a couple of kids,

06:22.360 --> 06:24.180
those were remarkably manageable

06:24.180 --> 06:26.620
on a single average median wage.

06:26.700 --> 06:26.920
Right.

06:26.920 --> 06:27.780
And to build on that,

06:27.860 --> 06:29.500
just look at millennials and Gen Z

06:29.500 --> 06:30.960
entering adulthood today.

06:31.840 --> 06:33.480
That structure hasn't just changed.

06:33.540 --> 06:35.660
It is flipped entirely upside down.

06:35.740 --> 06:36.140
It's wild.

06:36.380 --> 06:39.100
The things that are incredibly cheap now

06:39.100 --> 06:42.020
are the things that used to be rare luxuries.

06:42.740 --> 06:44.300
Food production is highly optimized.

06:45.060 --> 06:46.260
Travel is accessible.

06:46.720 --> 06:49.540
Technology is almost like practically given away.

06:49.740 --> 06:51.740
Yeah, you can buy a massive 60-inch

06:51.740 --> 06:52.800
high-definition television

06:52.800 --> 06:55.500
or hold a supercomputer in your pocket

06:55.500 --> 06:57.360
for a fraction of what they would have cost

06:57.360 --> 06:58.400
decades ago

06:58.400 --> 07:01.080
in real inflation-adjusted terms.

07:01.260 --> 07:01.720
Exactly.

07:01.860 --> 07:03.880
You can buy a flat-screen TV for 200 bucks.

07:04.060 --> 07:06.480
You can stream virtually every movie ever made

07:06.480 --> 07:07.560
for 15 bucks a month.

07:08.080 --> 07:08.380
But...

07:08.380 --> 07:08.600
Right.

07:08.780 --> 07:09.440
And here's the kicker.

07:09.680 --> 07:11.080
You need half a million dollars

07:11.080 --> 07:14.280
just to get a modest, leaky roof over your head

07:14.280 --> 07:16.320
in a city where the jobs actually are.

07:16.320 --> 07:19.740
The massive, immovable pillars of adult existence

07:19.740 --> 07:23.200
housing, education, healthcare, childcare

07:23.200 --> 07:27.080
have become disproportionately, overwhelmingly expensive.

07:27.700 --> 07:29.240
And when those pillars become that expensive

07:29.240 --> 07:31.860
wages alone can no longer bridge the gap.

07:31.980 --> 07:33.960
That is the core of the inheritocracy.

07:34.200 --> 07:36.520
The gap can only be bridged by pre-existing wealth.

07:36.760 --> 07:39.240
So I have to step in and push back here

07:39.240 --> 07:40.720
just on behalf of anyone listening

07:40.720 --> 07:42.380
who might be feeling a bit defensive right now.

07:42.540 --> 07:42.940
Fair enough.

07:43.200 --> 07:44.440
Especially if they are a parent

07:44.440 --> 07:45.500
who has helped out their kids.

07:45.500 --> 07:48.040
Is this book just laying out

07:48.040 --> 07:49.920
a massive capitalist conspiracy?

07:50.540 --> 07:52.900
Like, is Filby just launching an attack

07:52.900 --> 07:55.140
on boomer parents for hoarding all the wealth,

07:55.280 --> 07:57.780
pulling up the ladder, and ruining the economy?

07:58.120 --> 07:59.720
It's a vital question to ask

07:59.720 --> 08:01.800
because that is often how this discourse

08:01.800 --> 08:02.980
devolves online, right?

08:03.100 --> 08:05.320
But the text is very careful here.

08:05.480 --> 08:05.720
Okay.

08:05.980 --> 08:07.440
What's fascinating here is that

08:07.440 --> 08:09.560
Filby explicitly states

08:09.560 --> 08:11.180
she does not want this read

08:11.180 --> 08:14.000
as a flat moral indictment of family support.

08:14.000 --> 08:15.240
She doesn't blame the parents.

08:15.240 --> 08:15.860
Not at all.

08:15.940 --> 08:18.020
In fact, she calls the so-called

08:18.020 --> 08:19.520
bank of mom and dad,

08:19.660 --> 08:21.460
you know, parents helping with down payments,

08:21.700 --> 08:23.460
paying for weddings, subsidizing rent.

08:24.020 --> 08:25.760
She calls that an economic model

08:25.760 --> 08:27.840
built entirely on parental love,

08:27.920 --> 08:29.280
not malice or conspiracy.

08:29.600 --> 08:29.700
Right.

08:30.040 --> 08:31.540
She deeply acknowledges that

08:31.540 --> 08:33.180
boomer parents are just trying to do

08:33.180 --> 08:34.540
what literally any parent

08:34.540 --> 08:36.120
across human history wants to do,

08:36.340 --> 08:38.180
protect their children from a harsh world

08:38.180 --> 08:39.360
and give them a good life.

08:39.360 --> 08:40.800
But the collective result

08:40.800 --> 08:42.420
of that individual love

08:42.420 --> 08:44.300
multiplied by millions of families

08:44.300 --> 08:45.040
over decades

08:45.040 --> 08:47.420
is where the structural problem lies.

08:47.780 --> 08:48.300
Precisely.

08:48.780 --> 08:50.200
It's individual virtue

08:50.200 --> 08:52.320
aggregating into systemic failure.

08:52.980 --> 08:54.540
She has a very sharp,

08:54.800 --> 08:56.120
memorable phrase for it, actually.

08:56.360 --> 08:56.760
What is it?

08:56.920 --> 08:57.420
She says,

08:57.640 --> 08:58.760
boomers as a cohort

08:58.760 --> 08:59.860
have often been

08:59.860 --> 09:01.300
brilliant parents,

09:01.380 --> 09:02.360
but bad citizens.

09:03.140 --> 09:03.660
Oh, wow.

09:03.920 --> 09:04.580
Brilliant parents,

09:04.680 --> 09:05.520
but bad citizens.

09:05.740 --> 09:06.960
Meaning they have been

09:06.960 --> 09:08.120
incredibly generous,

09:08.660 --> 09:09.620
self-sacrificing,

09:09.760 --> 09:10.340
and protective

09:10.340 --> 09:12.100
toward their own individual children,

09:12.320 --> 09:13.440
but collectively

09:13.440 --> 09:14.820
as a voting bloc

09:14.820 --> 09:16.040
and an economic force.

09:16.080 --> 09:17.260
Through their voting patterns, right.

09:17.300 --> 09:17.520
Right.

09:17.920 --> 09:18.680
Through their support

09:18.680 --> 09:20.220
for restrictive zoning laws

09:20.220 --> 09:21.440
that block new housing,

09:21.980 --> 09:23.700
their protection of massive tax breaks

09:23.700 --> 09:24.840
for pensions and property,

09:25.260 --> 09:25.920
they've entrenched

09:25.920 --> 09:27.740
the very systemic inequality

09:27.740 --> 09:29.420
that their personal generosity

09:29.420 --> 09:30.720
is desperately trying

09:30.720 --> 09:32.160
to shield their own kids from.

09:32.160 --> 09:33.100
They are essentially

09:33.100 --> 09:33.940
bailing water

09:33.940 --> 09:35.300
out of their own family's boat

09:35.300 --> 09:36.360
while simultaneously

09:36.360 --> 09:37.560
voting for policies

09:37.560 --> 09:39.220
that drill holes

09:39.220 --> 09:39.760
in the hull

09:39.760 --> 09:40.780
of the entire ship.

09:40.920 --> 09:42.160
That's a great way to put it.

09:42.320 --> 09:43.420
And the author of the essay,

09:43.600 --> 09:43.940
Flyxion,

09:44.620 --> 09:46.360
brings in this brilliant,

09:46.640 --> 09:47.740
highly relatable analogy

09:47.740 --> 09:48.620
from a conversation

09:48.620 --> 09:49.600
with the comedian,

09:50.100 --> 09:50.660
Trevor Noah,

09:51.280 --> 09:53.120
to explain the compounding logic

09:53.120 --> 09:53.880
of this advantage.

09:54.040 --> 09:54.340
Yes,

09:54.480 --> 09:55.520
the video game analogy.

09:55.900 --> 09:56.580
Because a lot of people

09:56.580 --> 09:56.920
just say,

09:57.280 --> 09:57.460
well,

09:57.500 --> 09:58.300
the older generation

09:58.300 --> 09:59.300
just got a head start,

09:59.300 --> 10:00.300
but a head start

10:00.300 --> 10:01.240
implies everyone

10:01.240 --> 10:02.140
is running on the exact

10:02.140 --> 10:02.940
same track

10:02.940 --> 10:03.520
just starting

10:03.520 --> 10:04.380
at different times.

10:04.560 --> 10:05.360
The reality

10:05.360 --> 10:06.320
is much more skewed.

10:06.560 --> 10:07.820
It captures the structural

10:07.820 --> 10:09.080
compounding dynamic

10:09.080 --> 10:10.380
of wealth perfectly,

10:10.760 --> 10:11.800
far better than

10:11.800 --> 10:12.960
a simple racing metaphor.

10:13.420 --> 10:15.140
I love this analogy so much.

10:15.200 --> 10:16.820
So I want you to imagine

10:16.820 --> 10:18.380
a massive,

10:18.800 --> 10:19.320
persistent,

10:19.840 --> 10:21.140
multiplayer video game.

10:21.780 --> 10:23.060
Think like World of Warcraft

10:23.060 --> 10:24.020
or something similar.

10:24.140 --> 10:24.300
Okay,

10:24.400 --> 10:24.880
I'm with you.

10:24.880 --> 10:26.440
One group of players

10:26.440 --> 10:27.580
representing the boomers

10:27.580 --> 10:29.020
in this historical alignment

10:29.020 --> 10:30.400
gets early access

10:30.400 --> 10:31.360
to the game servers.

10:32.040 --> 10:33.620
They log in five years

10:33.620 --> 10:34.220
before the game

10:34.220 --> 10:35.020
is officially released

10:35.020 --> 10:35.520
to the public.

10:35.620 --> 10:36.460
So they have the whole map

10:36.460 --> 10:37.080
to themselves.

10:37.580 --> 10:38.020
Exactly.

10:38.600 --> 10:40.100
Because they are the only ones

10:40.100 --> 10:40.820
wandering around

10:40.820 --> 10:41.860
in this digital world,

10:42.060 --> 10:43.000
they can run around

10:43.000 --> 10:43.920
farming all the best

10:43.920 --> 10:45.100
resource spawn points.

10:45.380 --> 10:46.600
They acquire all the

10:46.600 --> 10:47.620
highest level equipment

10:47.620 --> 10:48.940
with zero competition.

10:49.360 --> 10:50.320
And they buy up

10:50.320 --> 10:52.040
all the prime virtual real estate

10:52.040 --> 10:52.680
in the center

10:52.680 --> 10:53.560
of the main cities.

10:53.560 --> 10:54.000
Yes.

10:54.580 --> 10:55.980
They establish an absolutely

10:55.980 --> 10:56.760
dominant,

10:57.060 --> 10:58.120
unassailable position

10:58.120 --> 10:59.860
before the general population

10:59.860 --> 11:00.560
is even allowed

11:00.560 --> 11:01.800
to create an account.

11:02.100 --> 11:03.660
And then the server

11:03.660 --> 11:04.740
officially opens.

11:04.960 --> 11:05.180
Right.

11:05.400 --> 11:06.760
The rest of the players,

11:06.920 --> 11:07.880
the younger generations,

11:08.100 --> 11:09.780
finally log in at level one,

11:09.920 --> 11:10.980
but they aren't just

11:10.980 --> 11:11.800
starting from zero

11:11.800 --> 11:12.600
in a pristine,

11:13.020 --> 11:13.760
empty world.

11:13.960 --> 11:14.300
No.

11:14.480 --> 11:15.540
They are entering a world

11:15.540 --> 11:16.240
that is already

11:16.240 --> 11:17.660
entirely owned,

11:18.400 --> 11:19.100
monetized,

11:19.320 --> 11:20.080
and controlled

11:20.080 --> 11:22.060
by the early access players.

11:22.180 --> 11:22.600
Exactly.

11:22.600 --> 11:23.560
The early players

11:23.560 --> 11:24.500
aren't just further

11:24.500 --> 11:25.200
ahead on the map.

11:25.600 --> 11:26.540
They effectively charge

11:26.540 --> 11:27.420
rent to everyone

11:27.420 --> 11:28.420
who arrives late.

11:28.900 --> 11:29.680
If you want a sword,

11:30.000 --> 11:30.680
you have to buy it

11:30.680 --> 11:31.500
from the early player

11:31.500 --> 11:33.080
who controls the iron mine.

11:33.460 --> 11:34.180
If you want a place

11:34.180 --> 11:34.700
to sleep,

11:35.020 --> 11:35.880
you have to rent it

11:35.880 --> 11:36.760
from the early player

11:36.760 --> 11:37.620
who owns the inn.

11:37.860 --> 11:39.140
The advantage isn't just

11:39.140 --> 11:40.320
that they started earlier.

11:40.480 --> 11:41.880
The advantage compounds

11:41.880 --> 11:42.840
exponentially,

11:43.160 --> 11:44.100
purely based on

11:44.100 --> 11:44.900
when they started.

11:45.060 --> 11:45.600
And it becomes

11:45.600 --> 11:46.320
structurally,

11:46.520 --> 11:47.040
mathematically,

11:47.260 --> 11:48.160
almost impossible

11:48.160 --> 11:49.140
for the latecomers

11:49.140 --> 11:50.340
to ever close that gap,

11:50.420 --> 11:51.180
no matter how hard

11:51.180 --> 11:51.700
they grind,

11:52.040 --> 11:52.760
no matter how many

11:52.760 --> 11:53.680
hours they play.

11:53.840 --> 11:54.620
Because the economy

11:54.620 --> 11:55.300
is rigged

11:55.300 --> 11:56.520
to funnel the fruits

11:56.520 --> 11:57.620
of their labor upwards

11:57.620 --> 11:59.200
to the property owners.

11:59.580 --> 12:00.280
It is a state

12:00.280 --> 12:01.720
of systemic lock-in.

12:02.140 --> 12:02.360
So,

12:02.580 --> 12:03.680
if we accept

12:03.680 --> 12:04.560
this diagnosis,

12:05.220 --> 12:05.980
that this massive

12:05.980 --> 12:07.000
generational wealth

12:07.000 --> 12:07.480
transfer

12:07.480 --> 12:08.720
is a real,

12:09.040 --> 12:10.120
measurable phenomenon,

12:10.620 --> 12:11.320
and that modern

12:11.320 --> 12:11.780
opportunity

12:11.780 --> 12:13.300
is increasingly tied

12:13.300 --> 12:13.780
to the bank

12:13.780 --> 12:14.560
of mom and dad,

12:14.940 --> 12:15.920
we are left

12:15.920 --> 12:16.820
with a massive

12:16.820 --> 12:18.040
mechanical puzzle.

12:18.740 --> 12:18.940
Right.

12:19.120 --> 12:19.600
Because

12:19.600 --> 12:21.080
inheritocracy

12:21.080 --> 12:22.020
explains the what,

12:22.280 --> 12:22.800
but we need

12:22.800 --> 12:23.300
to understand

12:23.300 --> 12:23.800
the how.

12:24.360 --> 12:25.320
How does a parent's

12:25.320 --> 12:25.900
wealth actually

12:25.900 --> 12:26.880
buy their child

12:26.880 --> 12:27.740
a good job

12:27.740 --> 12:28.640
in a supposedly

12:28.640 --> 12:30.180
meritocratic society?

12:30.320 --> 12:31.240
Because let's be real,

12:31.280 --> 12:31.680
it's not like

12:31.680 --> 12:32.520
it's 1850.

12:33.080 --> 12:33.660
Parents aren't

12:33.660 --> 12:34.240
generally just

12:34.240 --> 12:34.880
walking into

12:34.880 --> 12:35.840
a corporate headquarters,

12:36.080 --> 12:36.640
slapping down

12:36.640 --> 12:37.680
a leather briefcase

12:37.680 --> 12:38.740
full of non-sequential

12:38.740 --> 12:40.120
bills on a CEO's desk

12:40.120 --> 12:40.480
and saying,

12:40.880 --> 12:42.240
make my 22-year-old kid

12:42.240 --> 12:43.040
a vice president

12:43.040 --> 12:43.580
of marketing.

12:43.700 --> 12:43.900
Right.

12:44.120 --> 12:44.540
Overt,

12:44.760 --> 12:46.020
cartoonish nepotism

12:46.020 --> 12:46.460
exists,

12:46.640 --> 12:46.840
sure,

12:47.000 --> 12:47.580
but that's not

12:47.580 --> 12:48.080
how the bulk

12:48.080 --> 12:48.700
of the modern

12:48.700 --> 12:50.160
HR-regulated economy

12:50.160 --> 12:50.660
works.

12:50.780 --> 12:50.960
No,

12:51.060 --> 12:51.540
the mechanism

12:51.540 --> 12:52.220
is much more

12:52.220 --> 12:52.460
subtle,

12:52.580 --> 12:53.280
much more pervasive,

12:53.620 --> 12:54.320
and deeply,

12:54.780 --> 12:56.000
inextricably ingrained

12:56.000 --> 12:56.480
in the very

12:56.480 --> 12:57.520
institutions we trust.

12:57.680 --> 12:58.080
The wealth

12:58.080 --> 12:58.740
isn't buying

12:58.740 --> 12:59.640
the job directly,

13:00.120 --> 13:00.620
the wealth

13:00.620 --> 13:01.960
is buying proxies.

13:02.300 --> 13:03.160
And this brings us

13:03.160 --> 13:03.980
to the absolute

13:03.980 --> 13:04.820
core dilemma

13:04.820 --> 13:05.580
of this entire

13:05.580 --> 13:06.180
deep dive.

13:06.260 --> 13:06.780
The developmental

13:06.780 --> 13:08.060
opacity concept.

13:08.420 --> 13:08.700
Yes.

13:09.440 --> 13:09.980
The essay

13:09.980 --> 13:11.040
introduces its

13:11.040 --> 13:11.560
central,

13:12.000 --> 13:12.880
paradigm-shifting

13:12.880 --> 13:13.340
thesis.

13:14.020 --> 13:14.960
It is a heavy

13:14.960 --> 13:15.320
term,

13:15.640 --> 13:16.140
so let's break

13:16.140 --> 13:16.480
it down.

13:16.760 --> 13:17.260
What does this

13:17.260 --> 13:18.020
actually mean

13:18.020 --> 13:18.600
in practice?

13:19.560 --> 13:19.800
Well,

13:19.920 --> 13:20.800
the essay argues

13:20.800 --> 13:21.400
that when an

13:21.400 --> 13:22.000
institution,

13:22.360 --> 13:22.960
whether that's

13:22.960 --> 13:23.620
a corporate

13:23.620 --> 13:24.440
employer looking

13:24.440 --> 13:24.960
at your resume,

13:25.520 --> 13:26.120
a university

13:26.120 --> 13:26.960
admissions board

13:26.960 --> 13:27.940
reading your essay,

13:28.140 --> 13:28.920
or a state

13:28.920 --> 13:29.800
licensing board,

13:30.260 --> 13:31.080
when they evaluate

13:31.080 --> 13:31.440
you,

13:31.540 --> 13:32.120
they are never

13:32.120 --> 13:33.240
ever evaluating

13:33.240 --> 13:34.480
a finished person.

13:34.740 --> 13:35.680
This is such

13:35.680 --> 13:36.420
a critical,

13:36.680 --> 13:37.420
often overlooked

13:37.420 --> 13:38.380
insight about

13:38.380 --> 13:39.100
human capital.

13:39.820 --> 13:40.400
Human beings

13:40.400 --> 13:41.400
are not static

13:41.400 --> 13:41.960
objects.

13:41.960 --> 13:42.480
We are not

13:42.480 --> 13:43.140
machines that

13:43.140 --> 13:43.760
roll off an

13:43.760 --> 13:44.420
assembly line

13:44.420 --> 13:45.060
with a stamped

13:45.060 --> 13:45.580
spec sheet,

13:45.700 --> 13:45.820
right?

13:45.880 --> 13:46.100
Right.

13:46.480 --> 13:47.120
We are deeply

13:47.120 --> 13:48.060
path-dependent,

13:48.160 --> 13:49.080
highly unfinished,

13:49.560 --> 13:50.920
continuously evolving

13:50.920 --> 13:51.700
trajectories.

13:51.900 --> 13:52.760
And competence,

13:53.260 --> 13:53.620
true,

13:53.880 --> 13:54.140
deep,

13:54.380 --> 13:55.200
reliable competence

13:55.200 --> 13:56.260
in any complex

13:56.260 --> 13:56.960
modern field,

13:57.060 --> 13:57.840
has not yet fully

13:57.840 --> 13:58.640
manifested when you

13:58.640 --> 13:59.440
are young or early

13:59.440 --> 14:00.000
in your career.

14:00.140 --> 14:00.540
It is,

14:00.700 --> 14:01.320
by definition,

14:01.480 --> 14:02.560
developmentally opaque.

14:02.700 --> 14:03.300
We cannot see

14:03.300 --> 14:03.920
through the surface.

14:04.120 --> 14:04.680
We cannot look

14:04.680 --> 14:05.380
at a 20-year-old

14:05.380 --> 14:06.240
and know for certain

14:06.240 --> 14:07.200
what their true

14:07.200 --> 14:08.800
latent capability is,

14:08.800 --> 14:09.420
or how they will

14:09.420 --> 14:10.240
react under pressure

14:10.240 --> 14:11.320
five years from now.

14:11.760 --> 14:12.360
And more importantly,

14:12.520 --> 14:13.540
the author argues

14:13.540 --> 14:15.000
that we cannot just

14:15.000 --> 14:16.300
test people directly

14:16.300 --> 14:17.320
through unrestricted

14:17.320 --> 14:18.120
trial and error

14:18.120 --> 14:19.040
to find out,

14:19.340 --> 14:20.600
because doing so

14:20.600 --> 14:21.300
is inherently,

14:21.680 --> 14:22.980
profoundly unsafe.

14:23.340 --> 14:24.240
To really drive

14:24.240 --> 14:24.700
this home,

14:24.920 --> 14:25.480
let's bring in

14:25.480 --> 14:26.220
the specific,

14:26.620 --> 14:28.020
highly contrasting

14:28.020 --> 14:29.160
illustration used

14:29.160 --> 14:29.820
in the text.

14:29.960 --> 14:30.580
The programmer

14:30.580 --> 14:31.420
and the surgeon.

14:31.620 --> 14:31.920
Yes,

14:32.060 --> 14:33.020
it looks at two

14:33.020 --> 14:33.620
very different

14:33.620 --> 14:34.720
professions to show

14:34.720 --> 14:36.320
how society handles

14:36.320 --> 14:37.680
this unknown potential.

14:37.680 --> 14:38.740
The contrast

14:38.740 --> 14:39.900
between these two roles

14:39.900 --> 14:40.940
perfectly illustrates

14:40.940 --> 14:42.640
exactly why society

14:42.640 --> 14:43.620
is forced to rely

14:43.620 --> 14:44.420
on proxies.

14:44.980 --> 14:45.700
Let's start with

14:45.700 --> 14:46.560
the junior programmer.

14:47.080 --> 14:47.320
Okay.

14:47.680 --> 14:48.880
If you run a tech startup

14:48.880 --> 14:49.780
and you want to know

14:49.780 --> 14:50.500
if a young,

14:50.660 --> 14:51.600
untested programmer

14:51.600 --> 14:52.760
is actually competent,

14:53.060 --> 14:53.900
you can reveal

14:53.900 --> 14:54.640
that competence

14:54.640 --> 14:55.540
relatively quickly

14:55.540 --> 14:56.240
and directly.

14:56.420 --> 14:57.020
You just look at

14:57.020 --> 14:57.740
their public GitHub

14:57.740 --> 14:58.740
portfolio to see

14:58.740 --> 14:59.540
code they've already written?

14:59.780 --> 15:00.480
Or you can give them

15:00.480 --> 15:01.600
a two-hour technical

15:01.600 --> 15:02.480
coding test during

15:02.480 --> 15:02.960
the interview.

15:03.160 --> 15:03.800
Or, frankly,

15:03.960 --> 15:04.780
you can just hire them,

15:04.900 --> 15:05.660
give them a low-level

15:05.660 --> 15:06.380
bug to fix,

15:06.380 --> 15:07.520
and let them ship code

15:07.520 --> 15:08.260
for a few weeks

15:08.260 --> 15:09.100
to see what happens.

15:09.400 --> 15:09.980
And what happens

15:09.980 --> 15:10.540
if they fail?

15:11.260 --> 15:12.240
Let's say it turns out

15:12.240 --> 15:12.800
they are actually

15:12.800 --> 15:13.820
terrible at coding,

15:14.120 --> 15:15.280
they lied on their resume,

15:15.620 --> 15:16.580
they push an update,

15:16.920 --> 15:17.720
and the internal

15:17.720 --> 15:18.680
beta app crashes.

15:19.260 --> 15:20.680
The cost of that failure

15:20.680 --> 15:22.080
falls primarily,

15:22.320 --> 15:23.280
almost exclusively,

15:23.500 --> 15:24.120
on the programmer

15:24.120 --> 15:24.520
themselves.

15:24.700 --> 15:24.920
Right.

15:25.020 --> 15:25.480
They get pulled

15:25.480 --> 15:26.160
into a meeting,

15:26.580 --> 15:27.600
they might get fired,

15:27.740 --> 15:28.680
their career trajectory

15:28.680 --> 15:29.260
stalls,

15:29.720 --> 15:30.460
they get a terrible

15:30.460 --> 15:30.980
reference.

15:31.360 --> 15:32.300
The company might lose

15:32.300 --> 15:33.480
some development time,

15:33.600 --> 15:34.520
maybe a few thousand

15:34.520 --> 15:35.760
dollars in wasted payroll,

15:35.760 --> 15:37.120
but it is generally

15:37.120 --> 15:38.600
a highly contained,

15:39.080 --> 15:39.980
fully acceptable

15:39.980 --> 15:41.440
business risk.

15:41.780 --> 15:42.960
The systemic cost

15:42.960 --> 15:44.640
of early revelation-like

15:44.640 --> 15:46.020
finding out they are

15:46.020 --> 15:46.900
bad at their job

15:46.900 --> 15:48.660
is remarkably low.

15:48.900 --> 15:49.440
But now,

15:49.600 --> 15:50.900
let's flip that scenario

15:50.900 --> 15:52.000
entirely and look

15:52.000 --> 15:52.860
at the junior surgeon.

15:52.920 --> 15:53.400
Oh, this is where

15:53.400 --> 15:53.980
it gets scary.

15:54.620 --> 15:55.140
Symmetrically,

15:55.420 --> 15:56.380
a junior surgeon's

15:56.380 --> 15:57.500
competence could also

15:57.500 --> 15:58.740
be revealed very,

15:58.900 --> 15:59.340
very quickly.

15:59.580 --> 16:00.480
If you wanted to know

16:00.480 --> 16:01.200
if a medical school

16:01.200 --> 16:02.140
graduate was actually

16:02.140 --> 16:03.280
good with a scalpel,

16:03.700 --> 16:04.680
you could just hand it

16:04.680 --> 16:05.960
to them, wheel a patient

16:05.960 --> 16:07.040
into the operating room,

16:07.440 --> 16:08.120
let them perform

16:08.120 --> 16:09.800
a complex bypass surgery

16:09.800 --> 16:11.260
completely unsupervised,

16:11.440 --> 16:12.620
and observe the results.

16:13.000 --> 16:13.620
You would know

16:13.620 --> 16:14.580
within hours

16:14.580 --> 16:15.740
if they had the hands,

16:15.880 --> 16:16.220
the nerve,

16:16.300 --> 16:17.000
and the knowledge

16:17.000 --> 16:18.080
to be a great surgeon.

16:18.460 --> 16:19.700
But the cost of failure

16:19.700 --> 16:20.880
there is catastrophic.

16:21.320 --> 16:22.100
It is literal

16:22.100 --> 16:23.460
life and death.

16:23.700 --> 16:24.500
And critically,

16:24.800 --> 16:26.060
that catastrophic cost

16:26.060 --> 16:26.940
does not fall

16:26.940 --> 16:28.100
on the surgeon who failed.

16:28.420 --> 16:29.400
It falls on an

16:29.400 --> 16:30.320
unconsenting,

16:30.800 --> 16:31.780
vulnerable patient

16:31.780 --> 16:32.400
on the table.

16:32.540 --> 16:33.100
Wow, yeah.

16:33.160 --> 16:33.840
No institution,

16:34.180 --> 16:35.320
no hospital board,

16:35.480 --> 16:37.320
and no functioning society

16:37.320 --> 16:38.520
can ever afford

16:38.520 --> 16:39.080
to find out

16:39.080 --> 16:40.360
if someone is a good surgeon

16:40.360 --> 16:41.840
through unrestricted

16:41.840 --> 16:42.520
trial and error.

16:42.980 --> 16:44.040
The risk is simply

16:44.040 --> 16:45.040
too immense.

16:45.400 --> 16:46.700
Because human competence

16:46.700 --> 16:48.020
in these high-stakes fields

16:48.020 --> 16:49.400
takes an unusually

16:49.400 --> 16:50.420
long time to develop,

16:50.800 --> 16:51.840
and because it is often

16:51.840 --> 16:52.900
incredibly unsafe

16:52.900 --> 16:54.360
to observe that competence

16:54.360 --> 16:55.600
directly while it is

16:55.600 --> 16:56.220
still developing,

16:56.800 --> 16:57.840
society has no choice.

16:58.100 --> 16:58.780
It has to build

16:58.780 --> 16:59.860
elaborate workarounds.

16:59.860 --> 17:01.080
And those workarounds,

17:01.180 --> 17:02.060
the massive systems

17:02.060 --> 17:02.940
we've built to avoid

17:02.940 --> 17:04.340
the danger of trial and error,

17:04.560 --> 17:05.280
are the proxies.

17:05.360 --> 17:06.180
It's the credentials,

17:06.480 --> 17:07.480
the board certifications,

17:07.780 --> 17:08.380
the licenses,

17:09.040 --> 17:10.080
the four-year degrees,

17:10.700 --> 17:11.500
the decade-long

17:11.500 --> 17:13.000
supervised residencies.

17:13.140 --> 17:14.240
What's fascinating here

17:14.240 --> 17:15.220
is how this developmental

17:15.220 --> 17:16.500
opacity framework

17:16.500 --> 17:18.320
fundamentally redefines

17:18.320 --> 17:19.360
the entire purpose

17:19.360 --> 17:20.600
of our credentialing systems.

17:20.940 --> 17:22.120
In casual conversation,

17:22.280 --> 17:23.540
we often dismiss credentials

17:23.540 --> 17:25.700
as just bureaucratic red tape

17:25.700 --> 17:28.080
or like elitist status symbols

17:28.080 --> 17:29.760
or just HR box checking.

17:29.860 --> 17:30.660
Right, just jumping

17:30.660 --> 17:31.240
through hoops.

17:31.480 --> 17:33.400
This essay argues powerfully

17:33.400 --> 17:35.280
that they are structural necessities.

17:36.000 --> 17:38.020
They occupy a very narrow,

17:38.320 --> 17:39.580
highly fraught band

17:39.580 --> 17:40.860
between two massive

17:40.860 --> 17:42.400
societal failure modes.

17:42.580 --> 17:43.620
Let's lay out those

17:43.620 --> 17:44.420
two failure modes

17:44.420 --> 17:45.320
because this is crucial.

17:45.760 --> 17:46.740
The two ways society

17:46.740 --> 17:47.880
can get this wrong

17:47.880 --> 17:49.600
are testing too slowly

17:49.600 --> 17:51.140
and testing too fast.

17:51.460 --> 17:52.700
If you test too slowly,

17:53.220 --> 17:54.580
if you hold young people back

17:54.580 --> 17:55.600
for years and years,

17:56.000 --> 17:57.100
forcing them to jump through

17:57.100 --> 17:58.920
endless irrelevant academic hoops

17:58.920 --> 18:00.220
without ever letting them

18:00.220 --> 18:01.100
touch real work

18:01.100 --> 18:01.960
or prove themselves,

18:02.540 --> 18:03.880
you get mass disengagement.

18:03.880 --> 18:04.880
You get a generation

18:04.880 --> 18:06.520
of bored, frustrated people

18:06.520 --> 18:08.020
and you waste oceans

18:08.020 --> 18:08.960
of human potential.

18:09.240 --> 18:09.960
But on the other side

18:09.960 --> 18:10.380
of the spectrum,

18:10.520 --> 18:11.440
if you test too fast

18:11.440 --> 18:12.520
in a domain where failure

18:12.520 --> 18:13.800
is deeply costly,

18:14.280 --> 18:15.120
if you just let anyone

18:15.120 --> 18:16.600
practice law or medicine

18:16.600 --> 18:18.060
or structural engineering

18:18.060 --> 18:19.800
without rigorous prior vetting,

18:20.060 --> 18:21.380
you get catastrophic,

18:21.760 --> 18:22.740
irreversible harm

18:22.740 --> 18:23.360
to the public.

18:23.360 --> 18:24.740
So the proxy system,

18:25.200 --> 18:25.640
the credential,

18:25.800 --> 18:26.300
the degree,

18:27.120 --> 18:29.140
is society's flawed

18:29.140 --> 18:30.520
but necessary attempt

18:30.520 --> 18:31.120
to navigate

18:31.120 --> 18:34.060
between wasting human potential

18:34.060 --> 18:35.620
and causing absolute disaster.

18:35.740 --> 18:37.300
It's a really fine line to walk.

18:37.440 --> 18:38.460
Okay, let's unpack this

18:38.460 --> 18:39.140
with an analogy

18:39.140 --> 18:40.120
because I want to make sure

18:40.120 --> 18:41.060
you, the listener,

18:41.160 --> 18:42.180
can really visualize

18:42.180 --> 18:43.820
how this applies to your life

18:43.820 --> 18:45.920
outside of an extreme example

18:45.920 --> 18:47.660
like a hospital operating room.

18:47.700 --> 18:48.500
I love analogies.

18:48.580 --> 18:49.060
Let's hear it.

18:49.360 --> 18:51.200
Think about this massive problem

18:51.200 --> 18:52.540
like the process

18:52.540 --> 18:53.760
of buying a used car.

18:54.400 --> 18:55.280
You are standing

18:55.280 --> 18:56.240
in the dealership lot.

18:56.500 --> 18:57.500
You are looking at a sedan.

18:58.160 --> 18:59.620
And your primary concern is,

19:00.160 --> 19:00.980
is the transmission

19:00.980 --> 19:01.640
going to blow up

19:01.640 --> 19:02.720
the second I drive it

19:02.720 --> 19:03.540
a thousand miles

19:03.540 --> 19:04.160
down the highway?

19:04.540 --> 19:05.220
You want to know

19:05.220 --> 19:07.220
the true internal competence

19:07.220 --> 19:07.900
of that machine.

19:08.040 --> 19:08.440
Exactly.

19:08.880 --> 19:10.160
But you can't just tell the dealer,

19:10.460 --> 19:11.540
hey, hand me a wrench,

19:11.640 --> 19:12.960
let me completely dismantle

19:12.960 --> 19:13.620
the engine block

19:13.620 --> 19:14.720
and drop the transmission

19:14.720 --> 19:15.680
right here on the asphalt

19:15.680 --> 19:16.960
so I can personally inspect

19:16.960 --> 19:17.480
the gears

19:17.480 --> 19:18.620
to see if they're grinding.

19:18.840 --> 19:19.600
They would absolutely

19:19.600 --> 19:20.500
kick you off the lot.

19:20.740 --> 19:20.940
Right.

19:21.200 --> 19:21.900
It's too costly.

19:22.040 --> 19:22.760
It takes too much time.

19:22.840 --> 19:23.380
It's dangerous.

19:23.740 --> 19:24.480
And the dealer would call

19:24.480 --> 19:25.240
the cops on you.

19:25.980 --> 19:27.760
You cannot directly observe

19:27.760 --> 19:28.700
the internal mechanic

19:28.700 --> 19:29.300
of the car

19:29.300 --> 19:30.560
before you commit to it.

19:30.560 --> 19:32.400
So, faced with that opacity,

19:32.740 --> 19:33.440
what do you do

19:33.440 --> 19:34.600
as a rational buyer?

19:35.040 --> 19:36.060
You rely on a proxy.

19:36.840 --> 19:37.960
You walk around the car

19:37.960 --> 19:39.660
and you look for that shiny

19:39.660 --> 19:41.160
certified pre-owned sticker

19:41.160 --> 19:42.100
on the window.

19:42.380 --> 19:42.920
The sticker?

19:43.140 --> 19:44.660
You trust the dealership's

19:44.660 --> 19:45.760
stamp of approval.

19:46.560 --> 19:47.640
That sticker implies

19:47.640 --> 19:49.360
a massive background apparatus.

19:49.360 --> 19:51.920
It implies that someone,

19:52.480 --> 19:52.760
somewhere,

19:53.120 --> 19:54.040
in a safe environment,

19:54.680 --> 19:55.600
with the right tools,

19:55.960 --> 19:57.000
put the car on a lift,

19:57.320 --> 19:58.320
ran the diagnostics,

19:58.420 --> 19:59.200
and did the dangerous

19:59.200 --> 20:00.000
checking for you.

20:00.000 --> 20:01.120
And the essay is saying

20:01.120 --> 20:02.060
that modern society

20:02.060 --> 20:03.540
applies that exact same

20:03.540 --> 20:05.080
certified pre-owned logic

20:05.080 --> 20:06.300
to human beings.

20:06.300 --> 20:07.800
When you apply for a job,

20:08.040 --> 20:09.120
your Ivy League degree,

20:09.340 --> 20:10.580
your prestigious internship

20:10.580 --> 20:11.560
at Goldman Sachs,

20:11.660 --> 20:12.900
your glowing references,

20:13.340 --> 20:14.680
those are the certified pre-owned

20:14.680 --> 20:15.800
stickers on your window.

20:16.060 --> 20:17.040
They tell the employer,

20:17.320 --> 20:18.620
someone else already took the risk

20:18.620 --> 20:19.600
of testing this person,

20:19.720 --> 20:20.580
so you don't have to.

20:20.580 --> 20:22.600
It is a very robust,

20:22.920 --> 20:24.220
highly applicable way

20:24.220 --> 20:26.020
to conceptualize the problem.

20:26.640 --> 20:28.000
Society has to use

20:28.000 --> 20:29.060
these proxy stickers

20:29.060 --> 20:30.140
for safety,

20:30.620 --> 20:31.000
efficiency,

20:31.400 --> 20:32.720
and to keep the economy moving.

20:32.920 --> 20:33.220
However,

20:33.680 --> 20:34.740
the author of the essay

20:34.740 --> 20:36.420
is very intellectually honest.

20:36.580 --> 20:37.440
They are careful to note

20:37.440 --> 20:38.280
that this observation

20:38.280 --> 20:38.960
about credentials

20:38.960 --> 20:40.740
isn't entirely new,

20:40.880 --> 20:41.820
like in a vacuum.

20:42.200 --> 20:42.520
Right.

20:42.880 --> 20:44.340
The academic world economists,

20:44.600 --> 20:45.800
sociologists, philosophers,

20:45.980 --> 20:47.400
they've fiercely debated

20:47.400 --> 20:48.360
for decades about

20:48.360 --> 20:49.640
why these stickers exist,

20:49.640 --> 20:50.560
who gets them,

20:50.900 --> 20:51.660
and how they function.

20:51.920 --> 20:53.020
The essay actually spends

20:53.020 --> 20:54.420
a significant amount of time

20:54.420 --> 20:56.560
mapping out the existing landscape

20:56.560 --> 20:57.820
of academic theories

20:57.820 --> 20:59.180
to show exactly

20:59.180 --> 21:00.120
what this new

21:00.120 --> 21:01.860
developmental opacity framing

21:01.860 --> 21:02.680
actually adds

21:02.680 --> 21:04.420
to a very old conversation.

21:04.760 --> 21:05.040
Right.

21:05.160 --> 21:06.040
Because if you are

21:06.040 --> 21:07.040
an independent researcher

21:07.040 --> 21:08.420
going to drop a major new

21:08.420 --> 21:10.360
unified theory of inequality,

21:11.080 --> 21:12.160
you have to show your homework.

21:12.300 --> 21:12.960
You can't just pretend

21:12.960 --> 21:14.020
you were the first person

21:14.020 --> 21:15.040
to notice that degrees

21:15.040 --> 21:15.580
are expensive.

21:15.800 --> 21:16.420
You have to prove

21:16.420 --> 21:17.300
you know it came before.

21:17.580 --> 21:18.400
And the essay runs

21:18.400 --> 21:19.760
through an absolute gauntlet

21:19.760 --> 21:20.900
of heavy-hitting

21:20.900 --> 21:21.880
academic theories.

21:21.880 --> 21:22.840
I think we need

21:22.840 --> 21:23.620
to walk through them

21:23.620 --> 21:25.320
because they each offer

21:25.320 --> 21:26.700
a uniquely fascinating

21:26.700 --> 21:27.600
piece of the puzzle

21:27.600 --> 21:30.020
of why the working world

21:30.020 --> 21:31.240
functions the way it does.

21:31.700 --> 21:32.080
Let's start with

21:32.080 --> 21:33.140
the economic heavyweights.

21:33.620 --> 21:34.840
First is Michael Spence.

21:35.200 --> 21:36.500
His work is absolutely

21:36.500 --> 21:37.140
foundational

21:37.140 --> 21:38.660
to this entire discussion.

21:38.840 --> 21:39.880
The signaling model, right?

21:39.940 --> 21:40.280
Yes.

21:40.500 --> 21:41.820
In the 1970s,

21:42.000 --> 21:42.920
Spence proposed

21:42.920 --> 21:44.180
the signaling model.

21:44.700 --> 21:45.580
In labor economics,

21:45.580 --> 21:46.880
this theory explains

21:46.880 --> 21:48.220
why credentials function

21:48.220 --> 21:49.040
as costly

21:49.040 --> 21:50.420
and therefore credible

21:50.420 --> 21:52.300
signals under conditions

21:52.300 --> 21:53.740
of asymmetric information.

21:54.560 --> 21:55.300
Let's define that

21:55.300 --> 21:55.820
for a second.

21:56.520 --> 21:57.480
Asymmetric information

21:57.480 --> 21:58.500
just means that I,

21:58.620 --> 21:59.440
as the job applicant,

21:59.620 --> 22:00.880
know exactly how smart,

22:01.240 --> 22:01.840
hardworking,

22:01.980 --> 22:03.120
and reliable I am.

22:03.520 --> 22:04.080
But you,

22:04.560 --> 22:05.160
the employer,

22:05.620 --> 22:07.060
are completely in the dark.

22:07.640 --> 22:08.380
You have no idea.

22:08.660 --> 22:08.920
Correct.

22:09.140 --> 22:09.740
The information

22:09.740 --> 22:10.560
is unbalanced.

22:10.960 --> 22:11.900
And Spence argued

22:11.900 --> 22:12.800
something incredibly

22:12.800 --> 22:13.540
counterintuitive

22:13.540 --> 22:14.120
at the time.

22:14.400 --> 22:14.840
What was it?

22:14.840 --> 22:16.420
He argued that a degree,

22:16.760 --> 22:17.920
say, a grueling

22:17.920 --> 22:19.700
four-year university degree,

22:20.400 --> 22:21.360
is highly valuable

22:21.360 --> 22:22.060
to an employer

22:22.060 --> 22:22.860
not necessarily

22:22.860 --> 22:23.860
because of the specific

22:23.860 --> 22:24.680
knowledge you learned

22:24.680 --> 22:25.300
in the classroom.

22:25.460 --> 22:26.460
The employer doesn't care

22:26.460 --> 22:26.940
if you remember

22:26.940 --> 22:27.740
the dates of the

22:27.740 --> 22:28.420
French Revolution.

22:28.840 --> 22:29.280
Exactly.

22:29.740 --> 22:30.840
The degree is valuable

22:30.840 --> 22:31.960
because completing it

22:31.960 --> 22:32.480
was hard.

22:32.860 --> 22:33.520
It was costly

22:33.520 --> 22:34.440
in terms of time,

22:34.920 --> 22:35.820
immense effort,

22:36.360 --> 22:37.520
psychological pain,

22:37.620 --> 22:38.160
and money.

22:38.420 --> 22:39.460
So Spence's logic

22:39.460 --> 22:40.420
is that if you are

22:40.420 --> 22:41.340
naturally lazy,

22:41.640 --> 22:42.360
disorganized,

22:42.420 --> 22:43.360
or incompetent,

22:43.360 --> 22:45.000
the sheer cost

22:45.000 --> 22:45.660
and friction

22:45.660 --> 22:46.880
of getting the degree

22:46.880 --> 22:48.100
would be too high

22:48.100 --> 22:48.880
for you to endure.

22:49.320 --> 22:49.940
You would drop out.

22:50.620 --> 22:50.920
Therefore,

22:51.280 --> 22:52.220
the simple fact

22:52.220 --> 22:52.780
that you hold

22:52.780 --> 22:53.540
the piece of paper

22:53.540 --> 22:54.780
signals to the employer

22:54.780 --> 22:55.520
that you possess

22:55.520 --> 22:56.500
a certain baseline

22:56.500 --> 22:57.160
of grit,

22:57.580 --> 22:58.200
conformity,

22:58.400 --> 22:59.640
and general capability.

23:00.200 --> 23:01.260
So under Spence's

23:01.260 --> 23:01.920
signaling model,

23:02.300 --> 23:02.800
the university

23:02.800 --> 23:03.740
isn't really a place

23:03.740 --> 23:04.660
of profound learning.

23:04.860 --> 23:05.300
It's basically

23:05.300 --> 23:06.240
just an incredibly

23:06.240 --> 23:06.940
expensive

23:06.940 --> 23:08.120
four-year

23:08.120 --> 23:09.140
obstacle course

23:09.140 --> 23:10.220
designed to prove

23:10.220 --> 23:10.980
to corporations

23:10.980 --> 23:11.920
that you can jump

23:11.920 --> 23:12.380
through hoops

23:12.380 --> 23:13.040
without complaining.

23:13.400 --> 23:14.060
That is a deeply

23:14.060 --> 23:15.520
cynical but incredibly

23:15.520 --> 23:16.520
persuasive way

23:16.520 --> 23:17.120
to look at it.

23:17.220 --> 23:18.000
From economics,

23:18.280 --> 23:18.920
the essay then

23:18.920 --> 23:20.000
moves to sociology,

23:20.420 --> 23:20.860
bringing in

23:20.860 --> 23:21.520
Randall Collins.

23:21.920 --> 23:22.700
Collins looks at this

23:22.700 --> 23:23.420
from a completely

23:23.420 --> 23:24.020
different angle.

23:24.580 --> 23:25.440
He focuses on the

23:25.440 --> 23:26.060
phenomenon of

23:26.060 --> 23:26.940
credential inflation

23:26.940 --> 23:28.340
and social closure.

23:28.540 --> 23:29.380
Credential inflation.

23:29.540 --> 23:30.260
We see that everywhere.

23:30.620 --> 23:30.980
Everywhere.

23:31.540 --> 23:32.020
His theory

23:32.020 --> 23:33.160
brilliantly explains

23:33.160 --> 23:33.960
how credentialing

23:33.960 --> 23:35.040
systems naturally

23:35.040 --> 23:36.320
inevitably expand

23:36.320 --> 23:37.040
well beyond

23:37.040 --> 23:37.940
what any actual

23:37.940 --> 23:38.780
job functionally

23:38.780 --> 23:39.300
requires.

23:39.300 --> 23:40.200
Think about

23:40.200 --> 23:40.880
the generational

23:40.880 --> 23:41.360
shift.

23:41.880 --> 23:42.360
We all know

23:42.360 --> 23:43.140
of entry-level

23:43.140 --> 23:44.320
administrative jobs

23:44.320 --> 23:45.300
or basic sales

23:45.300 --> 23:46.460
positions that

23:46.460 --> 23:47.300
30 years ago

23:47.300 --> 23:48.320
perfectly functioning

23:48.320 --> 23:48.800
high school

23:48.800 --> 23:49.800
graduates filled.

23:50.140 --> 23:50.420
Today,

23:51.000 --> 23:52.180
those exact same

23:52.180 --> 23:53.320
jobs with the

23:53.320 --> 23:54.320
exact same

23:54.320 --> 23:55.300
daily tasks

23:55.300 --> 23:56.900
routinely demand

23:56.900 --> 23:57.880
a bachelor's degree

23:57.880 --> 23:58.800
or sometimes

23:58.800 --> 23:59.780
even a master's

23:59.780 --> 24:00.100
degree.

24:00.480 --> 24:01.140
Collins argues

24:01.140 --> 24:01.820
that credentials

24:01.820 --> 24:02.920
become geek-keeping

24:02.920 --> 24:03.840
mechanisms in their

24:03.840 --> 24:04.380
own right.

24:05.180 --> 24:05.800
They are a way

24:05.800 --> 24:06.600
for established

24:06.600 --> 24:07.520
professions to

24:07.520 --> 24:08.100
protect their

24:08.100 --> 24:09.040
elevated status,

24:09.300 --> 24:10.300
artificially limit

24:10.300 --> 24:10.880
competition,

24:11.240 --> 24:11.960
and keep the

24:11.960 --> 24:12.780
supply of labor

24:12.780 --> 24:13.580
low to keep

24:13.580 --> 24:14.000
their own

24:14.000 --> 24:14.720
wages high.

24:15.000 --> 24:15.640
It's the classic

24:15.640 --> 24:16.280
amusement park

24:16.280 --> 24:17.120
you-must-be-this-tall

24:17.120 --> 24:17.940
to-ride sign,

24:18.040 --> 24:18.400
but the people

24:18.400 --> 24:19.060
running the park

24:19.060 --> 24:19.940
keep moving the

24:19.940 --> 24:20.800
sign an inch

24:20.800 --> 24:21.580
higher every

24:21.580 --> 24:22.500
single year just

24:22.500 --> 24:22.960
to keep the

24:22.960 --> 24:23.640
lines manageable.

24:24.200 --> 24:24.720
That's exactly

24:24.720 --> 24:24.960
it.

24:25.260 --> 24:26.120
And then we get

24:26.120 --> 24:26.900
to Pierre Bourdieu,

24:27.000 --> 24:27.500
who I think is

24:27.500 --> 24:28.640
absolutely crucial for

24:28.640 --> 24:29.340
understanding the

24:29.340 --> 24:30.360
subtle, often

24:30.360 --> 24:31.360
invisible social

24:31.360 --> 24:32.440
dynamics of wealth.

24:32.620 --> 24:33.200
Bourdieu talks

24:33.200 --> 24:33.780
about capital,

24:33.900 --> 24:34.520
but he splits it

24:34.520 --> 24:35.420
into three types.

24:35.940 --> 24:36.360
Economic,

24:36.760 --> 24:37.100
cultural,

24:37.400 --> 24:38.000
and social.

24:38.000 --> 24:38.960
Bourdieu is

24:38.960 --> 24:39.840
essential reading

24:39.840 --> 24:41.140
because he explains

24:41.140 --> 24:42.080
how advantage

24:42.080 --> 24:43.720
reproduces across

24:43.720 --> 24:45.620
generations through

24:45.620 --> 24:46.620
mechanisms that go

24:46.620 --> 24:48.020
far, far beyond

24:48.020 --> 24:49.640
just direct financial

24:49.640 --> 24:50.500
transfers or

24:50.500 --> 24:51.360
inheritance checks.

24:51.720 --> 24:51.860
Right.

24:52.240 --> 24:53.080
Economic capital is

24:53.080 --> 24:53.920
money, obviously.

24:54.080 --> 24:54.880
We understand that.

24:55.280 --> 24:56.320
Social capital is who

24:56.320 --> 24:57.440
you know, your

24:57.440 --> 24:58.380
network, your

24:58.380 --> 24:58.820
parents'

24:58.960 --> 24:59.500
Rolodex, the

24:59.500 --> 25:00.200
fraternity you

25:00.200 --> 25:00.540
joined.

25:00.640 --> 25:01.540
But cultural capital

25:01.540 --> 25:02.220
is where it gets

25:02.220 --> 25:03.640
truly fascinating and

25:03.640 --> 25:04.820
deeply insidious.

25:05.160 --> 25:06.080
Cultural capital is

25:06.080 --> 25:07.300
knowing how to speak

25:07.300 --> 25:07.880
with a certain

25:07.880 --> 25:08.380
cadence.

25:09.180 --> 25:09.880
It's knowing how to

25:09.880 --> 25:11.160
dress for a specific

25:11.160 --> 25:11.660
room.

25:11.860 --> 25:12.780
It's knowing what

25:12.780 --> 25:14.240
obscure references to

25:14.240 --> 25:15.080
drop in an interview

25:15.080 --> 25:16.500
or what niche sports

25:16.500 --> 25:16.960
to play.

25:17.080 --> 25:17.940
It is a deeply

25:17.940 --> 25:19.220
internalized, almost

25:19.220 --> 25:20.520
subconscious code of

25:20.520 --> 25:21.980
behavior that signals

25:21.980 --> 25:23.040
to the people in power

25:23.040 --> 25:23.960
that you belong to

25:23.960 --> 25:25.040
their specific class.

25:25.260 --> 25:26.040
It's an incredibly

25:26.040 --> 25:27.560
powerful, entirely

25:27.560 --> 25:28.720
unspoken proxy.

25:28.900 --> 25:29.520
Let's do a thought

25:29.520 --> 25:30.380
experiment to really

25:30.380 --> 25:31.200
nail this down for the

25:31.200 --> 25:31.480
listener.

25:31.660 --> 25:32.100
Yes, please.

25:32.540 --> 25:33.240
Imagine you are

25:33.240 --> 25:34.240
applying for a highly

25:34.240 --> 25:35.420
competitive job at an

25:35.420 --> 25:36.080
elite management

25:36.080 --> 25:36.940
consulting firm.

25:37.360 --> 25:38.360
Two candidates make it

25:38.360 --> 25:39.200
to the final interview.

25:39.320 --> 25:40.200
Okay, candidate A

25:40.200 --> 25:40.920
and candidate B.

25:41.260 --> 25:41.500
Right.

25:42.020 --> 25:43.420
Candidate A grew up

25:43.420 --> 25:44.540
working class, went to

25:44.540 --> 25:45.820
a massive state school,

25:46.000 --> 25:47.080
ground out phenomenal

25:47.080 --> 25:48.640
grades, but has never

25:48.640 --> 25:49.400
been in a corporate

25:49.400 --> 25:50.060
boardroom.

25:50.220 --> 25:51.000
And candidate B.

25:51.320 --> 25:52.280
Candidate B grew up

25:52.280 --> 25:53.300
wealthy, their parents

25:53.300 --> 25:54.360
are executives, and

25:54.360 --> 25:54.940
they went to a prep

25:54.940 --> 25:55.180
school.

25:55.940 --> 25:56.500
During the interview

25:56.500 --> 25:57.620
small talk, the partner

25:57.620 --> 25:58.800
asks, so what do you

25:58.800 --> 25:59.560
like to do in the

25:59.560 --> 25:59.840
winter?

26:00.340 --> 26:01.580
Oh, I see where this is

26:01.580 --> 26:01.860
going.

26:02.200 --> 26:03.380
Candidate A talks about

26:03.380 --> 26:04.660
playing video games and

26:04.660 --> 26:05.920
catching up on sleep.

26:06.720 --> 26:07.980
Candidate B talks about

26:07.980 --> 26:09.460
skiing in Chamonix and

26:09.460 --> 26:10.920
mentions a specific type

26:10.920 --> 26:12.180
of binding they prefer.

26:12.440 --> 26:13.180
And the partner also

26:13.180 --> 26:14.020
skis in Chamonix.

26:14.120 --> 26:14.540
Exactly.

26:14.900 --> 26:15.360
Instantly.

26:15.720 --> 26:17.200
An invisible bond forms.

26:17.600 --> 26:18.720
The partner feels a sense

26:18.720 --> 26:20.140
of fit with candidate B.

26:20.360 --> 26:21.180
They speak the same

26:21.180 --> 26:21.680
language.

26:22.040 --> 26:23.300
So candidate B gets

26:23.300 --> 26:25.240
the job, not because

26:25.240 --> 26:26.320
they are better at Excel

26:26.320 --> 26:27.840
spreadsheets, but because

26:27.840 --> 26:28.860
their parents' wealth

26:28.860 --> 26:30.120
bought them the lived

26:30.120 --> 26:31.460
experiences that generate

26:31.460 --> 26:32.720
elite cultural capital.

26:32.720 --> 26:34.060
That is Bourdieu in a

26:34.060 --> 26:34.500
nutshell.

26:34.840 --> 26:36.100
The cultural capital acts

26:36.100 --> 26:37.580
as a proxy for competence

26:37.580 --> 26:38.920
because the interviewer

26:38.920 --> 26:40.100
conflates comfort and

26:40.100 --> 26:42.380
familiarity with capability.

26:42.900 --> 26:43.540
From Bourdieu's

26:43.540 --> 26:45.320
sociology, the essay then

26:45.320 --> 26:46.940
pivots back to a very

26:46.940 --> 26:48.500
specific data-heavy

26:48.500 --> 26:50.260
economic literature known

26:50.260 --> 26:51.700
as the employer learning

26:51.700 --> 26:52.540
literature.

26:52.720 --> 26:54.040
This brings in empirical

26:54.040 --> 26:55.600
researchers like Farber and

26:55.600 --> 26:57.040
Gibbons, whose work was

26:57.040 --> 26:58.820
later extended by Autonji and

26:58.820 --> 26:59.320
Puree.

26:59.320 --> 27:00.980
What is their specific angle

27:00.980 --> 27:02.100
on the proxy problem?

27:02.880 --> 27:03.740
The employer learning

27:03.740 --> 27:05.080
literature gets very, very

27:05.080 --> 27:05.920
close to the core

27:05.920 --> 27:06.980
developmental claim we are

27:06.980 --> 27:07.720
discussing today.

27:08.560 --> 27:09.600
Their economic models

27:09.600 --> 27:10.860
suggest that education

27:10.860 --> 27:12.880
functions as an early but

27:12.880 --> 27:14.740
highly imperfect signal of a

27:14.740 --> 27:15.560
worker's actual

27:15.560 --> 27:16.200
productivity.

27:16.580 --> 27:17.640
Basically, when you were

27:17.640 --> 27:18.780
first hired right out of

27:18.780 --> 27:20.360
college, your employer is

27:20.360 --> 27:21.460
flying completely blind.

27:21.580 --> 27:21.940
Exactly.

27:22.320 --> 27:23.060
They have to rely

27:23.060 --> 27:24.700
incredibly heavily on the

27:24.700 --> 27:26.180
prestige of your education

27:26.180 --> 27:27.540
to determine your starting

27:27.540 --> 27:29.020
wage because they just

27:29.020 --> 27:29.820
don't know you.

27:30.440 --> 27:31.720
But, over time, as you

27:31.720 --> 27:32.760
actually sit at your desk

27:32.760 --> 27:34.200
and work, the employer

27:34.200 --> 27:36.260
accumulates direct, daily

27:36.260 --> 27:37.840
observations of your actual

27:37.840 --> 27:38.460
performance.

27:38.600 --> 27:39.480
So the market learns.

27:39.640 --> 27:40.340
The market learns.

27:40.520 --> 27:42.140
The opacity slowly clears.

27:42.660 --> 27:43.880
And the data shows that as

27:43.880 --> 27:45.120
the employer learns your

27:45.120 --> 27:46.920
true underlying skill level,

27:47.360 --> 27:48.760
the weight they place on that

27:48.760 --> 27:50.340
initial educational signal

27:50.340 --> 27:51.300
decays.

27:51.720 --> 27:53.820
The halo effect of your fancy

27:53.820 --> 27:55.340
degree starts to evaporate.

27:55.340 --> 27:57.040
If you went to Harvard, but

27:57.040 --> 27:58.280
you are terrible at your

27:58.280 --> 28:00.000
job, they figure it out

28:00.000 --> 28:00.340
eventually.

28:00.520 --> 28:01.560
If you went to a community

28:01.560 --> 28:03.240
college, but you are an

28:03.240 --> 28:05.140
absolute genius, they figure

28:05.140 --> 28:05.740
that out too.

28:05.740 --> 28:07.720
The degree matters less and

28:07.720 --> 28:08.860
less the longer you are

28:08.860 --> 28:10.140
actually in the workforce

28:10.140 --> 28:11.180
producing measurable

28:11.180 --> 28:11.760
results.

28:11.940 --> 28:13.540
And finally, rounding out the

28:13.540 --> 28:15.160
academic landscape, the

28:15.160 --> 28:16.440
essay touches on a more

28:16.440 --> 28:17.880
normative moral theory.

28:18.400 --> 28:19.560
The philosopher Michael

28:19.560 --> 28:21.600
Sandel's stinging critique of

28:21.600 --> 28:23.020
meritocracy itself.

28:23.160 --> 28:24.300
Sandel argues that the

28:24.300 --> 28:26.140
rhetoric of meritocracy, the

28:26.140 --> 28:27.360
way we talk about it, is

28:27.360 --> 28:29.000
inherently damaging to the

28:29.000 --> 28:29.760
social fabric.

28:29.920 --> 28:30.400
It is.

28:30.520 --> 28:31.720
The very idea that the

28:31.720 --> 28:33.120
successful simply earned

28:33.120 --> 28:34.380
their position through pure

28:34.380 --> 28:36.000
hard work and raw talent

28:36.000 --> 28:37.900
generates a massive amount of

28:37.900 --> 28:39.040
hubris among the winners.

28:39.180 --> 28:40.060
They look down from the top

28:40.060 --> 28:40.760
of the mountain and think,

28:40.900 --> 28:42.420
I did this all myself.

28:42.800 --> 28:43.940
I deserve every penny.

28:44.160 --> 28:45.680
And symmetrically, it

28:45.680 --> 28:47.580
generates deep, crushing

28:47.580 --> 28:49.160
humiliation among those who

28:49.160 --> 28:50.080
are sorted to the bottom.

28:50.080 --> 28:52.220
Because if the system is truly

28:52.220 --> 28:53.540
meritocratic and you are at

28:53.540 --> 28:54.840
the bottom, the system is

28:54.840 --> 28:55.820
telling you that you belong

28:55.820 --> 28:56.480
there because you are

28:56.480 --> 28:57.580
fundamentally lesser.

28:58.080 --> 28:59.340
Sandel's critique is about

28:59.340 --> 29:01.540
the toxic moral posture that

29:01.540 --> 29:02.460
meritocratic sorting

29:02.460 --> 29:04.480
licenses, regardless of how

29:04.480 --> 29:06.360
statistically accurate that

29:06.360 --> 29:07.380
sorting actually is.

29:07.460 --> 29:08.700
So we have all these mass or

29:08.700 --> 29:09.560
established theories.

29:09.760 --> 29:11.240
We have Spence's signaling,

29:11.780 --> 29:12.660
Collins's inflation,

29:13.280 --> 29:14.480
Bourdieu's cultural capital,

29:14.860 --> 29:16.140
the employer learning decay,

29:16.620 --> 29:18.780
and Sandel's moral critique.

29:18.780 --> 29:20.100
And the author of the essay

29:20.100 --> 29:22.220
stops, takes a breath, and

29:22.220 --> 29:23.740
asks the vital question.

29:24.420 --> 29:25.660
If we already have all these

29:25.660 --> 29:26.980
brilliant explanations for

29:26.980 --> 29:29.020
why degrees matter, what

29:29.020 --> 29:30.300
does this new idea of

29:30.300 --> 29:32.040
developmental opacity actually

29:32.040 --> 29:32.980
add to the pile?

29:33.140 --> 29:34.380
Is it just a shiny new

29:34.380 --> 29:36.100
vocabulary word for old

29:36.100 --> 29:37.000
established facts?

29:37.100 --> 29:37.260
Right.

29:37.540 --> 29:38.480
And the essay argues

29:38.480 --> 29:39.780
forcefully that no, it's

29:39.780 --> 29:40.920
not just a rebranding.

29:41.160 --> 29:42.260
It adds a crucial,

29:42.720 --> 29:43.940
previously missing dimension

29:43.940 --> 29:44.880
to the mechanics of

29:44.880 --> 29:45.400
opportunity.

29:45.700 --> 29:46.820
If we look closely at the

29:46.820 --> 29:47.880
employer learning literature

29:47.880 --> 29:49.240
Farber, Gibbons, and the

29:49.240 --> 29:50.680
rest, their models are

29:50.680 --> 29:51.640
fundamentally, almost

29:51.640 --> 29:53.280
exclusively obsessed with

29:53.280 --> 29:54.440
the variable of speed.

29:54.840 --> 29:56.340
They are asking, how fast

29:56.340 --> 29:57.380
does the market learn your

29:57.380 --> 29:58.180
true productivity?

29:58.760 --> 29:59.940
How quickly does the signal

29:59.940 --> 30:01.440
of your degree fade over a

30:01.440 --> 30:02.300
five-year horizon?

30:02.620 --> 30:04.020
But the author of this essay

30:04.020 --> 30:05.800
argues that speed is not the

30:05.800 --> 30:07.640
only variable that matters in

30:07.640 --> 30:08.320
the real world.

30:08.460 --> 30:09.740
Here's the second axis.

30:10.560 --> 30:12.240
It's not just about how fast

30:12.240 --> 30:13.440
the market learns the truth.

30:13.440 --> 30:15.620
It is about who bears the

30:15.620 --> 30:18.080
terrifying cost of that early

30:18.080 --> 30:19.840
learning if the revelation

30:19.840 --> 30:20.860
proves to be poor.

30:21.640 --> 30:22.480
If you turn out to be

30:22.480 --> 30:23.980
terrible, who pays the price?

30:24.320 --> 30:24.640
Precisely.

30:25.020 --> 30:26.180
That is the breakthrough

30:26.180 --> 30:26.780
insight.

30:27.160 --> 30:29.200
The essay argues that treating

30:29.200 --> 30:31.180
speed as the only variable

30:31.180 --> 30:34.120
completely obscures the massive,

30:34.560 --> 30:35.920
multi-billion dollar

30:35.920 --> 30:38.020
institutional machinery that

30:38.020 --> 30:39.920
society has built specifically to

30:39.920 --> 30:41.540
manage the cost of failure.

30:41.540 --> 30:42.800
This is where the developmental

30:42.800 --> 30:44.520
framing connects beautifully to

30:44.520 --> 30:46.240
established education research.

30:47.000 --> 30:48.660
The author cites Lavin Wenger's

30:48.660 --> 30:50.260
concept of legitimate

30:50.260 --> 30:51.540
peripheral participation.

30:51.720 --> 30:52.900
Okay, that sounds like the

30:52.900 --> 30:54.900
most intense, impenetrable

30:54.900 --> 30:56.320
academic jargon possible.

30:56.400 --> 30:57.060
It really does.

30:57.260 --> 30:57.400
Yeah.

30:57.480 --> 30:58.480
Legitimate peripheral

30:58.480 --> 30:59.160
participation.

30:59.920 --> 31:01.180
What does that actually look like

31:01.180 --> 31:02.280
if you walk into a workplace

31:02.280 --> 31:03.160
in the real world?

31:03.260 --> 31:04.320
Think of it as the foundational

31:04.320 --> 31:05.760
architecture of apprenticeship.

31:06.440 --> 31:07.820
It is how human beings have

31:07.820 --> 31:09.360
transferred complex skills

31:09.360 --> 31:10.120
for millennia.

31:10.120 --> 31:11.780
When someone enters a highly

31:11.780 --> 31:13.780
complex, potentially dangerous

31:13.780 --> 31:16.040
field, they don't start at the

31:16.040 --> 31:16.980
dead center of the action.

31:17.220 --> 31:18.380
They start on the periphery.

31:18.960 --> 31:20.280
They are given legitimate,

31:20.640 --> 31:22.280
real, necessary work.

31:22.540 --> 31:24.600
But it is peripheral, deeply

31:24.600 --> 31:25.680
low stakes work.

31:25.860 --> 31:27.580
The master blacksmith lets the

31:27.580 --> 31:29.080
apprentice sweep the floor and

31:29.080 --> 31:29.840
stoke the fire.

31:30.240 --> 31:31.300
They don't let them forge the

31:31.300 --> 31:32.600
king's sword on day one.

31:32.760 --> 31:32.940
Right.

31:33.180 --> 31:34.360
As the apprentice demonstrates

31:34.360 --> 31:36.220
competence and builds trust over

31:36.220 --> 31:38.060
months and years, they are moved

31:38.060 --> 31:40.180
progressively, carefully closer

31:40.180 --> 31:40.800
to the center.

31:41.040 --> 31:42.440
Taking on more consequential,

31:42.680 --> 31:43.880
more visible, more dangerous

31:43.880 --> 31:44.320
roles.

31:44.840 --> 31:46.180
The entire system is designed to

31:46.180 --> 31:47.360
demand that competence be

31:47.360 --> 31:48.640
demonstrated under strictly

31:48.640 --> 31:51.180
contained risk before unsupervised

31:51.180 --> 31:52.720
trust is finally granted.

31:53.120 --> 31:54.480
And the essay mentions medical

31:54.480 --> 31:55.980
training specifically here to

31:55.980 --> 31:57.520
ground this, referencing

31:57.520 --> 31:59.100
entrustable professional

31:59.100 --> 32:00.060
activities or EPAs.

32:00.820 --> 32:01.120
Yes.

32:01.120 --> 32:02.640
If you look at modern medical

32:02.640 --> 32:04.380
residencies, the training is often

32:04.380 --> 32:06.140
rigorously structured around these

32:06.140 --> 32:06.860
EPAs.

32:06.980 --> 32:08.580
A resident isn't just evaluated

32:08.580 --> 32:10.300
on general fuzzy medical

32:10.300 --> 32:10.800
knowledge.

32:11.500 --> 32:12.960
They were evaluated task by

32:12.960 --> 32:14.100
specific task.

32:14.400 --> 32:15.680
Can they safely draw blood?

32:15.800 --> 32:16.080
Yes.

32:16.260 --> 32:17.200
OK, check that box.

32:17.760 --> 32:19.480
Can they safely perform a routine

32:19.480 --> 32:20.180
appendectomy?

32:20.580 --> 32:21.660
No, not yet.

32:21.880 --> 32:23.260
The attending physician watches

32:23.260 --> 32:25.300
them closely, intervening if they

32:25.300 --> 32:25.860
make a mistake.

32:26.080 --> 32:27.500
Once they repeatedly prove

32:27.500 --> 32:29.160
competence in a safe, fully

32:29.160 --> 32:30.900
observed environment, they are

32:30.900 --> 32:33.020
finally entrusted to do that

32:33.020 --> 32:34.560
specific activity alone.

32:34.560 --> 32:36.400
The entire multi-year

32:36.400 --> 32:37.860
residency system is built

32:37.860 --> 32:39.800
primarily to manage the

32:39.800 --> 32:41.460
catastrophic cost of the

32:41.460 --> 32:42.460
junior surgeon failing.

32:43.120 --> 32:44.360
So what does this all mean for

32:44.360 --> 32:45.320
you, the listener?

32:45.980 --> 32:47.360
Why does this deep dive into

32:47.360 --> 32:49.100
academic theory matter for your

32:49.100 --> 32:50.340
life and your career?

32:50.740 --> 32:52.100
It matters because it

32:52.100 --> 32:54.080
fundamentally shifts how we view

32:54.080 --> 32:55.320
the whole system of

32:55.320 --> 32:55.820
evaluation.

32:56.260 --> 32:57.500
The author is saying that human

32:57.500 --> 32:59.240
beings uniquely among animals

32:59.240 --> 33:01.160
have an unusually long period of

33:01.160 --> 33:02.080
dependent development.

33:02.080 --> 33:04.140
We take a long, long time to get

33:04.140 --> 33:05.060
good at things.

33:05.220 --> 33:06.920
And therefore, every society,

33:07.180 --> 33:08.400
whether it's a hyper-capitalist

33:08.400 --> 33:10.360
tech hub or a traditional

33:10.360 --> 33:12.400
agrarian village, has to build

33:12.400 --> 33:13.540
machinery to manage this

33:13.540 --> 33:13.980
transition.

33:14.460 --> 33:15.940
It's a species-level fact.

33:16.180 --> 33:18.200
The need for proxies is not just

33:18.200 --> 33:19.420
a quirk of modern corporate

33:19.420 --> 33:21.140
capitalism, and it's not just a

33:21.140 --> 33:22.620
specific medieval guild trying to

33:22.620 --> 33:23.400
protect its borders.

33:24.100 --> 33:26.380
Every society needs a safe,

33:26.620 --> 33:28.460
contained environment to test

33:28.460 --> 33:29.460
unfinished humans.

33:29.460 --> 33:31.680
To take that a step further, it

33:31.680 --> 33:33.180
scales the problem from a local

33:33.180 --> 33:34.980
labor market annoyance to a

33:34.980 --> 33:37.500
universal human constraint, which

33:37.500 --> 33:39.400
leads us seamlessly into the next

33:39.400 --> 33:40.880
core concept of the essay.

33:41.160 --> 33:42.820
If we accept the premise that

33:42.820 --> 33:44.760
society absolutely needs a safe,

33:44.960 --> 33:46.640
proxy-generating environment to

33:46.640 --> 33:48.400
test unfinished humans without

33:48.400 --> 33:50.800
risking disaster, the next logical

33:50.800 --> 33:52.860
question in an unequal society is,

33:53.240 --> 33:55.300
where do wealthy parents go to buy

33:55.300 --> 33:57.000
this incredibly valuable environment

33:57.000 --> 33:57.840
for their kids?

33:57.840 --> 33:59.440
The answer, of course, the most

33:59.440 --> 34:00.580
obvious answer in the Western

34:00.580 --> 34:02.680
world, is elite universities.

34:03.040 --> 34:04.420
Harvard, Oxford, Stanford.

34:04.700 --> 34:06.380
But here is where the essay pulls

34:06.380 --> 34:08.360
off a really brilliant analytical

34:08.360 --> 34:08.840
move.

34:09.220 --> 34:10.780
It argues that the general public,

34:10.880 --> 34:12.100
and even many economists,

34:12.680 --> 34:14.400
fundamentally misunderstand what an

34:14.400 --> 34:15.640
elite university is actually

34:15.640 --> 34:17.760
selling because of what it calls the

34:17.760 --> 34:19.000
unbundling problem.

34:19.380 --> 34:21.620
We casually treat a university degree

34:21.620 --> 34:23.620
as if it's one single monolithic

34:23.620 --> 34:24.040
thing.

34:24.120 --> 34:24.900
I got a degree.

34:24.900 --> 34:26.700
But elite institutions are actually

34:26.700 --> 34:29.040
performing at least four vastly

34:29.040 --> 34:30.860
distinct functions simultaneously,

34:31.140 --> 34:33.460
all bundled under one massive price

34:33.460 --> 34:33.800
tag.

34:33.940 --> 34:35.940
Yes, they are a complex bundle.

34:36.540 --> 34:38.240
Let's break down the four distinct

34:38.240 --> 34:39.340
components of that bundle.

34:40.060 --> 34:42.040
First, and most obviously, you have

34:42.040 --> 34:42.440
teaching.

34:42.820 --> 34:44.660
This is the actual instruction, the

34:44.660 --> 34:46.480
direct transmission of knowledge from

34:46.480 --> 34:48.220
a professor to a student in a

34:48.220 --> 34:49.600
classroom or a lecture hall.

34:49.760 --> 34:51.280
This is the thing universities put in

34:51.280 --> 34:52.480
all their glossy brochures.

34:52.480 --> 34:54.500
The picture of the distinguished

34:54.500 --> 34:56.380
professor pointing at a chalkboard

34:56.380 --> 34:58.580
while diverse students look on in

34:58.580 --> 34:58.980
awe.

34:59.220 --> 34:59.660
Exactly.

35:00.280 --> 35:01.920
But it is only a fraction of the

35:01.920 --> 35:02.240
value.

35:02.860 --> 35:04.760
Second, you have protected practice.

35:05.120 --> 35:06.300
Which directly solves the

35:06.300 --> 35:08.000
developmental opacity problem we just

35:08.000 --> 35:08.740
discussed at length.

35:09.000 --> 35:10.880
The elite university provides a safe,

35:11.220 --> 35:13.340
heavily subsidized, incredibly low

35:13.340 --> 35:15.180
stakes environment for profound trial

35:15.180 --> 35:15.600
and error.

35:15.600 --> 35:17.860
You can write a completely terrible,

35:18.100 --> 35:19.160
poorly argued essay.

35:19.580 --> 35:21.560
You can utterly fail a chemistry lab

35:21.560 --> 35:21.980
experiment.

35:22.520 --> 35:24.580
You can aggressively debate a highly

35:24.580 --> 35:26.280
controversial idea in a seminar.

35:26.760 --> 35:28.240
And the cost of that failure is

35:28.240 --> 35:29.800
contained entirely to a temporary

35:29.800 --> 35:31.320
letter grade on a piece of paper,

35:31.560 --> 35:33.480
not a catastrophic real world

35:33.480 --> 35:35.500
disaster or a fired career.

35:35.720 --> 35:37.060
It's the ultimate sandbox.

35:37.780 --> 35:39.120
You get to play, you get to

35:39.120 --> 35:40.980
experiment, and you get to fail

35:40.980 --> 35:43.340
safely, protected by ivy covered

35:43.340 --> 35:43.800
walls.

35:44.200 --> 35:45.440
Third in the bundle, you have

35:45.440 --> 35:46.120
certification.

35:46.760 --> 35:48.540
Certification is the institution

35:48.540 --> 35:51.160
formally observing your competence as

35:51.160 --> 35:52.780
you play in that protected environment

35:52.780 --> 35:54.840
and then formally stamping it with

35:54.840 --> 35:56.000
their institutional approval.

35:56.340 --> 35:58.260
It is the literal generation of the

35:58.260 --> 35:58.660
proxy.

35:59.140 --> 36:00.760
They are saying to the outside world,

36:00.880 --> 36:02.200
to the employers who weren't there,

36:02.580 --> 36:04.500
we watched them develop for four years,

36:04.820 --> 36:06.420
they meet our rigorous standards,

36:06.840 --> 36:07.700
here is the degree.

36:08.100 --> 36:09.060
You can trust them.

36:09.360 --> 36:11.060
And finally, the fourth and perhaps

36:11.060 --> 36:13.480
most economically powerful function of

36:13.480 --> 36:14.900
the bundle, networking.

36:15.500 --> 36:17.560
Elite universities effectively act as a

36:17.560 --> 36:19.040
geographic and social funnel,

36:19.040 --> 36:20.780
concentrating the future elites of

36:20.780 --> 36:23.720
finance, politics, tech, and law in one

36:23.720 --> 36:25.320
physical place for four years.

36:25.720 --> 36:27.340
The value of the late night dorm room

36:27.340 --> 36:29.620
relationships built there often has

36:29.620 --> 36:31.120
absolutely nothing to do with anything

36:31.120 --> 36:34.180
taught in the economics classroom, but it

36:34.180 --> 36:37.020
is immensely, staggeringly valuable for

36:37.020 --> 36:37.900
future opportunity.

36:38.460 --> 36:40.260
The essay brings in this fascinating,

36:40.500 --> 36:42.460
highly revealing anecdote about Bill

36:42.460 --> 36:44.980
Gates to illustrate this exact point.

36:45.360 --> 36:47.340
Now, the text is careful to note that this is

36:47.340 --> 36:49.480
based on widely reported interviews over

36:49.480 --> 36:52.120
the years, not a single peer-reviewed

36:52.120 --> 36:54.380
academic source, but the underlying

36:54.380 --> 36:56.680
logic perfectly mirrors the structural

36:56.680 --> 36:57.280
reality.

36:57.280 --> 36:59.360
Gates was asked by an interviewer about

36:59.360 --> 37:01.080
his stated plans to leave the vast

37:01.080 --> 37:03.360
majority of his immense, world-altering

37:03.360 --> 37:05.240
fortune to his charitable foundation,

37:05.460 --> 37:07.800
rather than giving a massive, direct

37:07.800 --> 37:09.580
financial inheritance to his children.

37:09.900 --> 37:11.660
And he reportedly said that his

37:11.660 --> 37:13.400
children's true inheritance was his name

37:13.400 --> 37:14.300
and his network.

37:14.300 --> 37:16.360
His reasoning, paraphrased, was that if

37:16.360 --> 37:18.180
they couldn't generate vast wealth with

37:18.180 --> 37:20.480
that level of access, being able to call

37:20.480 --> 37:22.440
any CEO or world leader on earth, they

37:22.440 --> 37:24.600
didn't really deserve his actual cash

37:24.600 --> 37:24.980
anyway.

37:25.240 --> 37:28.340
It is a striking, deeply honest admission

37:28.340 --> 37:29.940
from one of the architects of the

37:29.940 --> 37:31.020
modern economy.

37:31.620 --> 37:34.660
He is explicitly stating that the checkbook

37:34.660 --> 37:36.460
isn't the primary inheritance.

37:36.460 --> 37:39.180
The bundle of access is the true

37:39.180 --> 37:39.620
inheritance.

37:40.180 --> 37:42.360
The name, the network, the proximity to

37:42.360 --> 37:42.760
power.

37:42.960 --> 37:44.700
But I have to push back here, just on a

37:44.700 --> 37:46.240
practical, logistical level.

37:46.300 --> 37:46.700
Go push back.

37:46.900 --> 37:48.960
If I'm an HR director or an employer

37:48.960 --> 37:51.280
looking at a resume from a candidate

37:51.280 --> 37:53.340
who just graduated from an Ivy League

37:53.340 --> 37:56.540
school, how can I possibly tell what

37:56.540 --> 37:57.840
they are actually paying for?

37:57.960 --> 37:58.900
That's the problem.

37:59.040 --> 37:59.260
Right.

37:59.560 --> 38:01.260
How do I know if they're a genuine

38:01.260 --> 38:04.000
genius who deeply benefited from the

38:04.000 --> 38:05.440
incredible teaching and the protected

38:05.440 --> 38:07.540
practice, or if they are just a

38:07.540 --> 38:10.020
mediocre, well-connected kid who bought

38:10.020 --> 38:11.660
the prestige certification and the

38:11.660 --> 38:11.960
network?

38:12.020 --> 38:13.440
Because the money buying the purely

38:13.440 --> 38:15.560
superficial prestige signal looks

38:15.560 --> 38:17.360
exactly the same on a bank ledger as

38:17.360 --> 38:19.100
the money buying genuine competence

38:19.100 --> 38:19.500
building.

38:19.660 --> 38:20.060
Exactly.

38:20.280 --> 38:22.140
This is exactly the dilemma the author

38:22.140 --> 38:24.100
highlights, and it is why evaluating

38:24.100 --> 38:25.520
these systems is so difficult.

38:26.080 --> 38:28.020
It is the absolute core of the

38:28.020 --> 38:28.780
bundling problem.

38:28.940 --> 38:30.880
Development, the actual teaching and

38:30.880 --> 38:32.700
the protected practice is where

38:32.700 --> 38:34.080
competence becomes real.

38:34.620 --> 38:36.320
That is the internal process where you

38:36.320 --> 38:38.320
actually get better at thinking and

38:38.320 --> 38:39.020
doing things.

38:39.020 --> 38:42.100
But certification and networking are how

38:42.100 --> 38:44.400
that internal reality is transmitted to

38:44.400 --> 38:46.600
third parties who weren't there to

38:46.600 --> 38:47.520
watch you grow.

38:47.520 --> 38:48.000
Right.

38:48.080 --> 38:49.540
The employer wasn't sitting in the back

38:49.540 --> 38:51.200
of the seminar room taking notes on your

38:51.200 --> 38:51.680
brilliance.

38:52.000 --> 38:52.940
They just see the resume.

38:53.460 --> 38:53.940
Exactly.

38:54.360 --> 38:56.500
And because the internal development and

38:56.500 --> 38:58.320
the external certification happened in the

38:58.320 --> 39:01.180
exact same physical building administered by

39:01.180 --> 39:03.840
the exact same institution and paid for with

39:03.840 --> 39:07.040
the exact same tuition check, separating them

39:07.040 --> 39:09.080
empirically is a logistical nightmare.

39:09.080 --> 39:11.720
From the outside looking in, you simply

39:11.720 --> 39:13.560
cannot untangle the threads.

39:14.120 --> 39:16.420
You cannot distinguish the noble investment in

39:16.420 --> 39:18.980
actual skill development from the cynical

39:18.980 --> 39:22.280
investment in the recognized gatekeeping signal.

39:22.280 --> 39:24.720
To bring back our earlier analogy, it's like

39:24.720 --> 39:27.220
buying our certified pre-owned car, but the

39:27.220 --> 39:29.300
dealership that sells it to you is also the

39:29.300 --> 39:30.900
factory that originally built the car.

39:30.900 --> 39:33.620
And they are also the regulatory agency that

39:33.620 --> 39:34.500
inspects the car.

39:34.660 --> 39:36.740
And they also run the incredibly exclusive

39:36.740 --> 39:38.680
country club you get to join if you buy the

39:38.680 --> 39:38.900
car.

39:39.220 --> 39:42.300
It's all rolled into one massive, opaque,

39:42.900 --> 39:44.320
deeply expensive package.

39:44.560 --> 39:46.680
Since unbundling this is so incredibly

39:46.680 --> 39:48.660
difficult, what happens when economists

39:48.660 --> 39:50.800
actually try to cut through the noise and

39:50.800 --> 39:51.800
look at the hard data?

39:52.100 --> 39:53.980
We need to look at the empirical reality of

39:53.980 --> 39:54.920
the prestige premium.

39:55.300 --> 39:57.360
Does an elite degree actually make you better,

39:57.720 --> 39:58.980
more productive and smarter?

39:58.980 --> 40:01.940
Or is it purely just a fancy sticker you buy?

40:02.240 --> 40:02.920
To skip the line.

40:03.340 --> 40:05.380
It is the multi-million dollar question at the

40:05.380 --> 40:06.380
heart of higher education.

40:06.900 --> 40:08.380
And the empirical literature the essay

40:08.380 --> 40:10.800
meticulously examines provides an answer that

40:10.800 --> 40:13.740
is wonderfully nuanced, highly complex, and

40:13.740 --> 40:15.700
frankly, perhaps a bit frustrating for anyone

40:15.700 --> 40:17.820
wanting a simple black and white yes or no.

40:18.140 --> 40:20.820
The essay dives into several major large-scale

40:20.820 --> 40:24.080
studies on elite university earnings premiums to

40:24.080 --> 40:27.660
see if the prestige sticker truly adds value, or if

40:27.660 --> 40:29.280
it's just a massive sorting artifact.

40:29.820 --> 40:32.280
Let's define sorting artifacts so we are all on the

40:32.280 --> 40:32.800
same page.

40:32.800 --> 40:35.480
That essentially means the university just acted

40:35.480 --> 40:38.320
like a giant vacuum, scooped up all the smartest,

40:38.540 --> 40:41.020
most driven, wealthiest 18-year-olds in the country,

40:41.480 --> 40:43.660
didn't really do anything to change them over four

40:43.660 --> 40:46.380
years, and then took all the credit when those

40:46.380 --> 40:48.780
highly capable people naturally went on to be

40:48.780 --> 40:50.020
successful at 30.

40:50.320 --> 40:51.360
Let's look at the numbers.

40:51.960 --> 40:54.380
The essay walks through a fascinating study by

40:54.380 --> 40:58.040
researchers Walker and Zhu using massive sets of UK

40:58.040 --> 40:59.060
labor force data.

40:59.660 --> 41:02.720
They looked at the raw wage return slope on selectivity.

41:02.900 --> 41:04.720
Let's translate that economic jargon.

41:05.040 --> 41:07.180
They simply looked at how much more money people made

41:07.180 --> 41:09.680
based on how highly selective and hard to get into their

41:09.680 --> 41:10.580
university was.

41:10.660 --> 41:13.500
And the raw, unadjusted number they found was a slope

41:13.500 --> 41:18.380
of 0.197, meaning yes, unconditionally, there is a very

41:18.380 --> 41:22.080
clear, very steep financial premium for going to a highly

41:22.080 --> 41:24.720
selective school, on average, you make significantly more

41:24.720 --> 41:25.020
money.

41:25.260 --> 41:27.300
But then they did something crucial with the data.

41:27.820 --> 41:28.700
They adjusted it.

41:28.780 --> 41:29.260
They did.

41:29.700 --> 41:32.080
Walker and Zhu controlled for the selectivity of the

41:32.080 --> 41:35.060
institutions that the student could have attended based on

41:35.060 --> 41:37.400
their prior academic record and test scores.

41:37.560 --> 41:40.160
In other words, they statistically accounted for the

41:40.160 --> 41:43.340
fact that elite universities admit students who were already

41:43.340 --> 41:47.780
incredibly smart, highly driven, and likely well-resourced

41:47.780 --> 41:49.800
before they ever stepped foot on campus.

41:49.800 --> 41:52.800
They isolated students who were already on a massive upward

41:52.800 --> 41:55.640
trajectory in life, regardless of whether they went to Oxford

41:55.640 --> 41:57.640
or a local state college.

41:57.980 --> 42:00.720
And when they applied that statistical control, when they

42:00.720 --> 42:04.260
accounted for how smart the kid was before the university got

42:04.260 --> 42:07.540
their hands on them, what happened to the massive prestige

42:07.540 --> 42:07.940
premium?

42:08.220 --> 42:09.600
It absolutely collapsed.

42:09.940 --> 42:14.280
The slope dropped from 0.197 down to 0.069.

42:14.600 --> 42:16.600
It was reduced by roughly two-thirds.

42:16.760 --> 42:16.980
Wow.

42:17.180 --> 42:18.280
Wait, let that sink in.

42:18.280 --> 42:21.860
Two-thirds of the perceived financial benefit, two-thirds of

42:21.860 --> 42:25.400
the magic of going to an elite school, vanished into thin air

42:25.400 --> 42:28.380
the moment you mathematically accounted for how capable the

42:28.380 --> 42:30.700
kid was before they even paid their first tuition bill.

42:31.100 --> 42:31.900
That is staggering.

42:32.160 --> 42:35.360
It suggests the university is taking credit for the raw material,

42:35.620 --> 42:37.160
not the value they added to it.

42:37.280 --> 42:39.740
And the essay backs this up with another major study by

42:39.740 --> 42:40.620
Chevalier and Conlon.

42:40.840 --> 42:41.080
Yes.

42:41.200 --> 42:43.500
Chevalier and Conlon took a slightly different approach.

42:43.500 --> 42:47.660
They compared the post-graduation wages of students from the highly

42:47.660 --> 42:51.920
prestigious Russell Group universities in the UK, their equivalent of the

42:51.920 --> 42:57.580
Ivy League, to graduates of post-1992 universities, which are generally

42:57.580 --> 43:00.800
viewed as less prestigious and less selective.

43:01.120 --> 43:01.660
What did they find?

43:02.000 --> 43:06.520
They found a raw 6% wage premium for the elite Russell Group schools.

43:06.520 --> 43:10.340
But again, when they dug into the data, they found that this premium was mostly

43:10.340 --> 43:15.140
explained by intake selectivity, the raw quality and background of the students

43:15.140 --> 43:19.140
coming in, rather than anything magically distinctive about the education they

43:19.140 --> 43:21.520
receive while sitting in those historic lecture halls.

43:21.640 --> 43:26.400
And here is a really critical, deeply revealing detail the author points out about that

43:26.400 --> 43:27.260
specific study.

43:28.020 --> 43:32.920
Chevalier and Conlon drew their wage estimates from salaries observed right after graduation.

43:32.920 --> 43:35.420
Why does that specific timing matter so much?

43:35.800 --> 43:41.460
Because right after graduation and your first year on the job is the exact window of time

43:41.460 --> 43:43.960
when employers are flying completely blind.

43:44.460 --> 43:49.160
They have absolutely nothing else to go on besides the institutional signal itself.

43:49.560 --> 43:53.380
The employer hasn't had time to observe the young workers' actual performance,

43:53.560 --> 43:54.680
work ethic, or intelligence.

43:55.320 --> 43:59.840
They are relying purely 100% on the proxy sticker.

43:59.840 --> 44:04.880
And yet, even in that initial window where the pure signaling effect of the proxy should

44:04.880 --> 44:10.260
theoretically be at its absolute strongest, intake selectivity still explains most of the

44:10.260 --> 44:10.600
premium.

44:11.000 --> 44:14.860
Okay, so looking at this data, it's easy to jump to the conclusion that it's all a scam,

44:15.160 --> 44:15.400
right?

44:15.640 --> 44:19.880
The universities actually do nothing, they add no educational value, and it's all just an

44:19.880 --> 44:24.200
incredibly elaborate, deeply expensive sorting mechanism for the already privileged.

44:24.760 --> 44:25.320
Not quite.

44:25.320 --> 44:30.320
If we connect this to the bigger picture, the essay is very careful to avoid falling into

44:30.320 --> 44:31.680
that reductionist trap.

44:32.000 --> 44:35.820
It points out that the system doesn't entirely collapse into pure cynical signaling.

44:36.240 --> 44:40.260
It brings in a third piece of evidence, a study by Sullivan et al., that found a very

44:40.260 --> 44:42.240
important, highly specific nuance.

44:42.640 --> 44:48.160
When you look at longitudinal data tracking careers over decades, even after rigorously controlling

44:48.160 --> 44:53.840
for socioeconomic background, parental wealth, and the type of secondary school attended,

44:54.320 --> 45:00.360
there is still a powerful residual association between holding an elite degree and reaching

45:00.360 --> 45:03.140
the absolute top tails of the income distribution.

45:03.840 --> 45:04.440
The top tails.

45:04.780 --> 45:07.260
Meaning, we aren't talking about middle management anymore.

45:07.460 --> 45:09.220
We are talking about the stratosphere.

45:09.840 --> 45:16.740
The mega-rich, the Fortune 500 CEOs, the top-tier law firm partners, the leading politicians.

45:16.740 --> 45:17.340
Yes.

45:17.820 --> 45:22.860
The elite school might not give you a massive, life-changing wage boost over a solid state

45:22.860 --> 45:26.940
school if your goal is just to be a competent, mid-level regional manager.

45:27.060 --> 45:31.780
But if you want to reach the absolute apex of wealth and systemic power, the elite degree

45:31.780 --> 45:34.540
still shows a massive, highly measurable effect.

45:34.800 --> 45:39.960
The data ultimately proves it is neither pure empty signaling nor pure magical value add.

45:40.440 --> 45:43.520
It is a deeply complex and tangled mixture of both.

45:43.580 --> 45:44.880
Here's where it gets really interesting.

45:44.880 --> 45:47.600
Let me try to translate this complex data for the listener.

45:47.640 --> 45:47.980
Please do.

45:48.260 --> 45:49.020
Think of it this way.

45:49.900 --> 45:54.540
The elite university doesn't necessarily take an average mind and magically forge it into

45:54.540 --> 45:55.320
a super genius.

45:56.040 --> 46:02.440
What the university really does is act as an intensely rigorous, incredibly trusted selection

46:02.440 --> 46:04.420
filter for the broader economy.

46:04.680 --> 46:10.200
It proves to the future employer that you were a genius, or at least highly capable and conforming,

46:10.200 --> 46:14.620
before you got there, simply because you survived their brutal admissions filter.

46:15.080 --> 46:19.940
But, and this is the absolute kicker that drives the inheritocracy, you still had to pay

46:19.940 --> 46:25.340
the university hundreds of thousands of dollars or leverage massive social capital to prove

46:25.340 --> 46:25.880
it for you.

46:25.920 --> 46:29.540
You have to buy access to the filter so the filter can tell the world how smart you are.

46:29.660 --> 46:30.020
Exactly.

46:30.300 --> 46:34.240
The system is functioning exactly as it was designed to function, to mitigate risk.

46:34.240 --> 46:38.440
But the design inherently requires immense resources to participate in.

46:38.680 --> 46:41.000
Which brings us to the crucial turning point of the essay.

46:42.020 --> 46:46.540
If we understand that the system is effectively rigged by institutions demanding these incredibly

46:46.540 --> 46:52.160
expensive wealth-gated proxies, how do we actually prove that's the real mechanism driving

46:52.160 --> 46:52.660
inequality?

46:53.080 --> 46:54.140
We change the test.

46:54.900 --> 47:01.220
This is where the author relocates the entire diagnosis of the inequality problem moving beyond

47:01.220 --> 47:02.780
Filby's initial premise.

47:02.780 --> 47:08.460
The argument is that wealth matters primarily because it buys access to recognized proxies.

47:09.120 --> 47:14.380
Wealth buys the unpaid internships in London or New York that you can afford to take because

47:14.380 --> 47:15.780
your parents pay your rent.

47:15.980 --> 47:21.320
Wealth buys the private health care and tutoring to keep you in top academic shape.

47:21.800 --> 47:24.260
And of course, wealth buys the elite degrees.

47:24.540 --> 47:29.840
But if reliance on these specific proxies is the real mechanism driving the gap, as opposed

47:29.840 --> 47:33.960
to just parents handing kids bags of cash, then this is a falsifiable theory.

47:34.400 --> 47:36.540
We can test it in the real world to see if it holds up.

47:36.620 --> 47:41.180
And the logic of the test the author proposes is beautifully, elegantly simple.

47:41.180 --> 47:46.280
If the author is wrong, and the inequality we see is only about direct family wealth transfers,

47:46.760 --> 47:50.460
then the only way to fix inequality is to heavily tax and redistribute wealth.

47:50.700 --> 47:52.780
Giving people money would be the only effective solution.

47:53.060 --> 47:57.660
But if the author is right, and the core root problem is institutional reliance on proxies

47:57.660 --> 47:59.780
to manage the fear of developmental opacity.

47:59.780 --> 48:05.660
Then changing how we assess people at the point of entry should dramatically reduce the effects

48:05.660 --> 48:10.540
of their background, even if the underlying wealth in society remains totally unequal.

48:10.880 --> 48:13.940
If you change the lens the institution uses to look at the candidate,

48:14.420 --> 48:17.320
the picture of who is capable should change.

48:17.760 --> 48:19.720
And the text points to real-world,

48:19.960 --> 48:24.600
ongoing attempts by economists and HR professionals to do exactly this.

48:25.040 --> 48:25.700
Audit studies.

48:25.980 --> 48:26.780
Let's talk about these.

48:26.780 --> 48:32.860
Have you, the listener, ever been part of a modern hiring process that utilized blind resume reviews?

48:33.040 --> 48:36.960
Where they systematically stripped the candidate's name, their gender, their address,

48:37.080 --> 48:42.480
and crucially, the name of their university off the page before the hiring manager is ever allowed to see it.

48:42.700 --> 48:42.980
Yes.

48:43.220 --> 48:47.200
Or, even more rigorously, the implementation of structured work sample testing.

48:47.500 --> 48:51.280
This is where, instead of sitting across a desk and asking someone where they went to school

48:51.280 --> 48:52.680
and what their greatest weakness is,

48:52.680 --> 48:59.200
you give them a paid, two-hour task that perfectly mimics the actual daily job they will be doing.

48:59.360 --> 49:00.720
If they are a coder, they code.

49:01.040 --> 49:03.340
If they are a marketer, they write a mock campaign.

49:03.820 --> 49:06.880
You evaluate the raw output, not the pedigree.

49:07.060 --> 49:09.360
Or the use of highly standardized interviews,

49:09.700 --> 49:14.240
where every single candidate is asked the exact same questions in the exact same order,

49:14.560 --> 49:17.060
evaluated against a rigid, predefined rubric.

49:17.060 --> 49:24.440
This is explicitly designed to prevent the interviewer from just having a chat to see if the candidate is a culture fit,

49:24.640 --> 49:27.920
which, as we know from Bourdieu's theory of cultural capital,

49:28.200 --> 49:33.000
is almost always just a subconscious test for shared elite background and comfort.

49:33.520 --> 49:35.360
I want to dive into this with a hypothetical,

49:35.660 --> 49:39.800
because I think it exposes the incredible psychological friction of this problem.

49:39.860 --> 49:40.600
Okay, let's hear it.

49:40.820 --> 49:43.020
Imagine you are a hiring manager.

49:43.640 --> 49:45.240
You have two candidates for a critical role.

49:45.960 --> 49:47.180
Candidate A went to Yale.

49:47.540 --> 49:50.060
They have a pristine resume, the perfect proxy sticker.

49:50.180 --> 49:50.480
Okay.

49:50.720 --> 49:52.880
But you give them a blind work sample test,

49:53.100 --> 49:55.160
and their output is, well, it's just okay.

49:56.120 --> 49:56.560
Mediocre.

49:56.760 --> 49:57.800
And candidate B?

49:58.040 --> 50:00.860
Candidate B went to a relatively unknown state school.

50:01.120 --> 50:02.340
Maybe they have a gap in their resume.

50:02.780 --> 50:05.060
But their work sample test is absolutely brilliant.

50:05.160 --> 50:06.120
It blows you away.

50:06.200 --> 50:07.060
Which one do you hire?

50:07.560 --> 50:09.120
The data says you should hire B.

50:09.120 --> 50:11.480
But the human psychology of risk aversion

50:11.480 --> 50:14.760
screams at you to hire Yale candidate A.

50:15.240 --> 50:17.520
Because if Yale candidate A fails on the job,

50:18.000 --> 50:19.000
you can tell your boss,

50:19.560 --> 50:20.600
hey, I hired the kid from Yale.

50:20.680 --> 50:21.720
How was I supposed to know?

50:22.200 --> 50:24.560
But if state school candidate B fails,

50:24.900 --> 50:25.700
your boss says,

50:25.960 --> 50:28.100
why did you take a massive risk on an unknown?

50:28.600 --> 50:31.200
The proxy is a shield for the hiring manager, too.

50:31.420 --> 50:32.640
That is exactly the friction.

50:32.760 --> 50:35.180
The proxy manages risk for everyone involved.

50:35.180 --> 50:39.340
Now, the essay notes that the findings of these real-world audit studies are mixed.

50:39.340 --> 50:44.240
Sometimes the effect sizes of blind hiring are smaller than progressive advocates hope.

50:44.800 --> 50:49.000
It's not a magic bullet that completely erases structural inequality overnight.

50:49.280 --> 50:55.280
But they represent real, tangible, incredibly important attempts to bypass the traditional proxy machinery.

50:55.960 --> 50:58.700
They are deliberate attempts to look directly at the raw competence,

50:59.180 --> 51:02.380
rather than the expensive, wealth-gated sticker on the window.

51:02.380 --> 51:05.280
And to illustrate how deep this proxy reliance goes,

51:05.520 --> 51:10.460
the text offers a brilliant, almost darkly comedic micro-illustration of the problem,

51:10.680 --> 51:13.780
pulled directly from Filby's book, Looking at the Financial Advice Sector.

51:13.980 --> 51:18.360
It is a perfect, crystalline paradox of how wealth gates access.

51:18.740 --> 51:19.540
It truly is.

51:19.920 --> 51:22.240
Filby notes that within the massive financial industry,

51:22.740 --> 51:28.860
there is a second, highly attentive, incredibly useful, bespoke tier of financial advice available.

51:28.860 --> 51:31.280
This tier doesn't just sell you mutual funds.

51:31.840 --> 51:34.220
It actually teaches you the deep mechanics of capital.

51:34.740 --> 51:38.300
It teaches you how to structure wealth, how to legally leverage assets,

51:38.600 --> 51:43.460
how to utilize complex tax vehicles, how to truly generationally grow money.

51:43.620 --> 51:44.740
It sounds amazing.

51:45.420 --> 51:47.500
But what is the catch for the average person?

51:47.660 --> 51:51.220
The catch is that this elite second tier of advice only activates,

51:51.680 --> 51:56.120
and is only made available after a client already possesses massive, substantial assets.

51:56.120 --> 52:01.240
The institution literally, structurally gates access to the vital information

52:01.240 --> 52:05.000
on how to build wealth behind the rigid prerequisite of already having wealth.

52:05.280 --> 52:07.820
It is the proxy problem, perfectly crystallized.

52:08.340 --> 52:12.940
The industry explicitly meant to help people manage and grow money relies on pre-existing wealth

52:12.940 --> 52:16.940
as the sole proxy for whether a client is worth their time and expertise to educate.

52:17.240 --> 52:21.320
I want you, the listener, to stop and think about your own workplace right now.

52:21.860 --> 52:23.660
Think about the last person your team hired,

52:23.660 --> 52:26.060
or the exact process of how you were hired.

52:26.560 --> 52:28.300
Do you use structured work samples?

52:28.560 --> 52:31.080
Do you rigorously test the actual skill?

52:31.340 --> 52:34.480
Or do you just glance at where they went to college, who refer them,

52:34.800 --> 52:37.480
and if they seem like a good fit, over a coffee chat?

52:37.640 --> 52:40.620
Because if it's the latter, and for most of us it is,

52:41.120 --> 52:45.600
you might be actively, if unknowingly, participating in the proxy problem.

52:45.780 --> 52:47.820
You might be relying on the very machinery

52:47.820 --> 52:52.240
that entrenches the generational inequality we've been talking about for the last hour.

52:52.240 --> 52:55.920
It is a sobering reflection for anyone in a position of authority.

52:56.500 --> 52:59.360
And as we move into the final analytical sections of the essay,

52:59.560 --> 53:01.720
the author broadens the scope even further.

53:02.380 --> 53:06.500
Up until now, we've been looking at this primarily through the massive lens of

53:06.500 --> 53:09.620
generations and class baby boomers versus millennials,

53:10.060 --> 53:12.980
the inherited wealthy versus the wage-earning working class.

53:12.980 --> 53:17.020
But the essay argues powerfully that this developmental opacity problem,

53:17.440 --> 53:21.000
this deeply flawed institutional reliance on legible proxies,

53:21.580 --> 53:24.380
fractures along other massive demographic lines as well.

53:24.480 --> 53:26.240
The invisible nuances of the system.

53:26.620 --> 53:29.700
The text explores two major massive complications

53:29.700 --> 53:32.960
to this simple parent-to-child wealth transfer model,

53:33.600 --> 53:34.700
gender and culture.

53:35.040 --> 53:35.900
Let's start with gender,

53:36.260 --> 53:38.980
because the demographic numbers here are absolutely wild

53:38.980 --> 53:40.800
and rarely talked about in this context.

53:40.800 --> 53:44.180
Filby identifies a massive unfolding phenomenon

53:44.180 --> 53:47.440
that she calls the great gender wealth transfer.

53:47.640 --> 53:48.940
And this is happening right now,

53:49.040 --> 53:51.640
right alongside the generational transfer we've been discussing.

53:51.880 --> 53:54.360
The central statistic she highlights is staggering.

53:55.340 --> 53:56.600
In the United States right now,

53:56.920 --> 54:01.520
spousal inheritance averages roughly $1.4 million per event.

54:01.800 --> 54:04.300
$1.4 million moving hands.

54:04.460 --> 54:04.760
Yes.

54:04.760 --> 54:08.500
And because of basic demographic life expectancies,

54:09.020 --> 54:11.600
specifically the fact that women, on average,

54:12.080 --> 54:14.220
tend to significantly outlive their husbands,

54:14.780 --> 54:17.420
this massive, unprecedented transfer of wealth

54:17.420 --> 54:20.200
flows disproportionately, overwhelmingly to women.

54:20.360 --> 54:23.140
So you have a huge, growing demographic of women

54:23.140 --> 54:27.440
coming into immense, highly liquid wealth later in their lives.

54:27.500 --> 54:30.620
But here is where the institutional proxy failure kicks in again,

54:30.660 --> 54:31.440
and it's maddening.

54:31.440 --> 54:33.800
You would logically think that the wealth management

54:33.800 --> 54:36.560
and financial advisory industry would be bending over backwards,

54:36.760 --> 54:38.360
shifting their entire business models

54:38.360 --> 54:41.020
to cater to this massive influx of female capital.

54:41.260 --> 54:41.860
But they aren't.

54:41.900 --> 54:43.700
They aren't, because the financial industry

54:43.700 --> 54:46.120
is still fundamentally stubbornly structured

54:46.120 --> 54:49.560
around the historical assumption of a male breadwinner.

54:49.760 --> 54:51.600
Their entire institutional machinery,

54:52.040 --> 54:53.860
their marketing, their risk assessment models,

54:53.980 --> 54:55.300
their communication styles,

54:55.300 --> 54:59.300
It all relies on an outdated, rigid proxy

54:59.300 --> 55:02.000
of what an economically relevant decision-maker

55:02.000 --> 55:03.820
is supposed to look and sound like.

55:04.020 --> 55:07.180
Because these women do not match the institutional proxy,

55:07.700 --> 55:10.280
the institution systematically misidentifies

55:10.280 --> 55:12.740
who actually holds the power in the capital.

55:12.940 --> 55:13.980
And as a direct result,

55:14.340 --> 55:16.360
these women, possessing millions of dollars,

55:16.840 --> 55:19.000
are left systematically ignored,

55:19.600 --> 55:21.200
patronized, and underserved

55:21.200 --> 55:23.920
by the very industry built to capture wealth.

55:23.920 --> 55:26.020
It is a profound failure of legibility.

55:26.480 --> 55:28.260
The institution literally cannot see them

55:28.260 --> 55:29.980
because their proxy filter is broken.

55:30.160 --> 55:30.660
It's incredible.

55:31.020 --> 55:33.680
The proxy is actively blinding the institution

55:33.680 --> 55:36.040
to a massive financial reality.

55:36.520 --> 55:38.660
It shows how rigid these systems become.

55:38.860 --> 55:41.420
And the second major complication the essay explores

55:41.420 --> 55:43.380
is cross-cultural variation.

55:43.800 --> 55:45.740
How do different societies and different nations

55:45.740 --> 55:48.340
handle the public acknowledgement of this inheritocracy?

55:48.740 --> 55:50.880
Because the underlying mechanical problem

55:50.880 --> 55:53.540
of evaluating potential might be universal.

55:54.220 --> 55:55.580
But the way we talk about it,

55:55.620 --> 55:56.980
the stories we tell ourselves,

55:57.340 --> 55:59.300
are vastly, wildly different.

55:59.600 --> 56:01.240
The essay does a brilliant job

56:01.240 --> 56:03.520
comparing three distinct cultural approaches.

56:03.940 --> 56:05.240
Let's start with Mumbai, India.

56:05.780 --> 56:07.240
Filby conducted interviews there,

56:07.380 --> 56:08.880
and the people she spoke with

56:08.880 --> 56:12.260
described a multi-generational financial contract

56:12.260 --> 56:14.500
that is treated as simply obvious,

56:14.760 --> 56:16.080
almost comparable to gravity.

56:16.080 --> 56:18.060
Wealth flows down from the parents

56:18.060 --> 56:19.680
to the children to establish them.

56:20.180 --> 56:21.160
And then, inevitably,

56:21.600 --> 56:22.960
care and financial support

56:22.960 --> 56:24.340
flow back up from the children

56:24.340 --> 56:26.080
to the parents in their old age.

56:26.180 --> 56:26.660
It's open.

56:26.940 --> 56:27.700
It's an accepted,

56:28.000 --> 56:30.440
openly discussed background fact of life.

56:30.700 --> 56:31.980
No one is hiding the fact

56:31.980 --> 56:33.020
that their family helped them.

56:33.100 --> 56:33.580
Exactly.

56:33.900 --> 56:35.380
It is legible to the culture.

56:35.700 --> 56:36.920
Now, contrast that with Britain.

56:37.520 --> 56:38.920
Filby notes that in the UK,

56:39.180 --> 56:40.360
the culture treats inheritance,

56:40.820 --> 56:43.120
trusts, and familial financial support

56:43.120 --> 56:44.060
with immense,

56:44.180 --> 56:46.680
almost pathological shame and concealment.

56:46.880 --> 56:48.320
People will bend over backward,

56:48.800 --> 56:50.180
invent elaborate fictions,

56:50.380 --> 56:51.940
and twist themselves into knots

56:51.940 --> 56:53.880
to hide the fact that their parents

56:53.880 --> 56:55.600
provided the deposit for their flat.

56:56.560 --> 56:57.420
The wealth is there,

56:57.840 --> 57:00.020
but the culture demands it remain invisible.

57:00.660 --> 57:01.740
And then you have America,

57:01.980 --> 57:03.920
which is always its own unique beast.

57:04.420 --> 57:06.060
The essay describes American culture

57:06.060 --> 57:08.400
as uniquely, aggressively aspirational

57:08.400 --> 57:10.360
about the concept of generational wealth

57:10.360 --> 57:12.500
as a stated, noble goal.

57:12.500 --> 57:13.580
People rap about it.

57:13.640 --> 57:15.640
They write millions of self-help books about it.

57:15.720 --> 57:17.620
They make endless internet content

57:17.620 --> 57:19.100
about building it for their descendants.

57:19.500 --> 57:21.120
It's seen as the ultimate pursuit.

57:21.480 --> 57:24.120
Yet, simultaneously and incredibly paradoxically,

57:24.600 --> 57:25.800
Americans aggressively,

57:26.000 --> 57:26.620
almost violently,

57:26.840 --> 57:28.580
downplay any inherited advantages

57:28.580 --> 57:29.940
they actually received in practice.

57:30.560 --> 57:32.940
They do this because they are culturally desperate

57:32.940 --> 57:34.420
to uphold the foundational,

57:35.120 --> 57:36.300
semi-religious myth

57:36.300 --> 57:39.320
of the pure, rugged, self-made individual.

57:39.720 --> 57:41.060
This raises an important question

57:41.060 --> 57:42.000
about universality.

57:42.000 --> 57:43.920
The cultural scripts we use

57:43.920 --> 57:46.420
to justify, celebrate, or hide the system

57:46.420 --> 57:48.360
change wildly across borders.

57:48.760 --> 57:50.740
But the underlying mechanical problem,

57:51.320 --> 57:53.220
the reality of developmental opacity,

57:53.800 --> 57:55.380
the immense danger of testing

57:55.380 --> 57:56.900
unproven human competence,

57:57.700 --> 57:59.600
and the rigid institutional demand

57:59.600 --> 58:00.980
for legible proxies,

58:01.920 --> 58:04.500
that is a universal constant

58:04.500 --> 58:06.160
across the human species.

58:06.320 --> 58:07.780
The machinery is churning everywhere.

58:07.940 --> 58:09.160
We just decorate the outside

58:09.160 --> 58:10.180
of the machine differently

58:10.180 --> 58:11.380
depending on where we live.

58:11.380 --> 58:13.500
We all fundamentally want to leave our kids

58:13.500 --> 58:15.000
a better, safer life.

58:15.540 --> 58:16.180
But in the West,

58:16.300 --> 58:17.680
we desperately want our neighbors

58:17.680 --> 58:19.740
to think our kids achieved it all by themselves,

58:20.060 --> 58:22.060
using nothing but raw grit,

58:22.520 --> 58:23.200
elbow grease,

58:23.560 --> 58:24.960
and undeniable talent.

58:25.160 --> 58:27.180
The irony and the cognitive dissonance

58:27.180 --> 58:28.280
are just thick.

58:28.520 --> 58:29.580
And I think that realization

58:29.580 --> 58:30.980
brings us to a natural point

58:30.980 --> 58:32.600
to pull all these complex

58:32.600 --> 58:33.620
weaving threads together.

58:33.620 --> 58:35.520
We need to render a final verdict

58:35.520 --> 58:36.840
on the core argument

58:36.840 --> 58:37.940
of the inheritocracy.

58:38.400 --> 58:39.900
Let's summarize the analytical journey

58:39.900 --> 58:40.820
of this deep dive.

58:41.400 --> 58:42.860
The author of the essay concludes,

58:43.200 --> 58:45.140
after reviewing all the academic models

58:45.140 --> 58:46.100
and empirical data,

58:46.620 --> 58:48.200
that Eliza Filby is absolutely,

58:48.520 --> 58:50.300
demonstrably right about the growing

58:50.300 --> 58:51.820
distributional pattern of wealth.

58:51.900 --> 58:53.080
Opportunity is, in fact,

58:53.160 --> 58:55.000
increasingly tied to family resources

58:55.000 --> 58:57.400
rather than raw, unsupported merit.

58:57.660 --> 58:59.620
But the essay's massive contribution

58:59.620 --> 59:01.860
is showing that inheritance alone,

59:01.860 --> 59:03.900
just the simple act of handing a child

59:03.900 --> 59:04.600
a bag of money

59:04.600 --> 59:07.420
isn't the true, deep mechanism at play.

59:07.840 --> 59:08.580
The real mechanism,

59:08.740 --> 59:10.440
the engine of modern inequality,

59:10.760 --> 59:13.000
is the complex, inevitable interaction

59:13.000 --> 59:14.500
between two massive forces.

59:15.100 --> 59:17.460
First, genuine developmental opacity.

59:18.160 --> 59:19.920
The hard fact that human competence

59:19.920 --> 59:21.540
takes a very long time to build

59:21.540 --> 59:23.860
and is fundamentally systemically unsafe

59:23.860 --> 59:25.080
to test in the real world

59:25.080 --> 59:26.700
through unfiltered trial and error.

59:27.200 --> 59:29.040
Second, the massive, rigid,

59:29.200 --> 59:30.740
institutional proxy systems

59:30.740 --> 59:31.880
we've built to manage

59:31.880 --> 59:33.280
that exact danger.

59:33.700 --> 59:34.700
The expensive degrees,

59:34.960 --> 59:36.020
the unpaid internships,

59:36.340 --> 59:37.940
the elite, gated networks.

59:38.440 --> 59:39.720
Wealth simply buys access

59:39.720 --> 59:40.700
to the recognized,

59:40.980 --> 59:42.600
institutionally trusted proxies.

59:42.900 --> 59:44.780
The deeper, more intractable problem

59:44.780 --> 59:46.040
isn't just the sad absence

59:46.040 --> 59:47.420
of a perfect meritocracy.

59:47.660 --> 59:48.460
It is the realization

59:48.460 --> 59:49.880
that managing human uncertainty

59:49.880 --> 59:51.640
absolutely requires proxies,

59:51.760 --> 59:52.860
and those vital proxies

59:52.860 --> 59:54.260
have been almost entirely captured

59:54.260 --> 59:54.940
and monetized

59:54.940 --> 59:56.060
by pre-existing wealth.

59:56.060 --> 59:58.100
So, as you go back to your day,

59:58.420 --> 59:59.880
as you head into your next meeting

59:59.880 --> 01:00:01.700
or look over the next stack of resumes,

01:00:01.840 --> 01:00:02.940
I want you to remember this.

01:00:03.880 --> 01:00:06.700
Every single time you are evaluated,

01:00:07.080 --> 01:00:08.080
and every single time

01:00:08.080 --> 01:00:09.380
you evaluate someone else,

01:00:09.580 --> 01:00:10.540
you are navigating

01:00:10.540 --> 01:00:12.900
this exact massive machinery.

01:00:13.440 --> 01:00:14.740
Your resume isn't just

01:00:14.740 --> 01:00:16.440
a dry list of things you've done.

01:00:16.740 --> 01:00:18.960
It is a highly engineered proxy,

01:00:19.580 --> 01:00:21.080
desperately attempting to manage

01:00:21.080 --> 01:00:22.320
the profound danger

01:00:22.320 --> 01:00:23.920
of your unknown potential

01:00:23.920 --> 01:00:25.780
for the anxious person reading it.

01:00:26.260 --> 01:00:26.980
And if we look forward

01:00:26.980 --> 01:00:27.540
to the future,

01:00:27.700 --> 01:00:28.600
the essay leaves us

01:00:28.600 --> 01:00:29.540
with a profound,

01:00:29.880 --> 01:00:31.040
deeply unsettling,

01:00:31.280 --> 01:00:32.060
open question

01:00:32.060 --> 01:00:33.500
about the nature of power

01:00:33.500 --> 01:00:34.580
in the 21st century.

01:00:34.720 --> 01:00:36.280
Who ultimately controls

01:00:36.280 --> 01:00:37.660
the machinery that decides

01:00:37.660 --> 01:00:38.980
which human capacities

01:00:38.980 --> 01:00:39.840
become legible

01:00:39.840 --> 01:00:41.100
and which remain ignored?

01:00:41.400 --> 01:00:42.360
And here's a final

01:00:42.360 --> 01:00:43.220
provocative thought

01:00:43.220 --> 01:00:43.980
for you to mull over,

01:00:44.300 --> 01:00:45.820
building on that exact question

01:00:45.820 --> 01:00:46.900
about the future of power.

01:00:47.180 --> 01:00:48.280
We have established today

01:00:48.280 --> 01:00:49.660
that elite institutions,

01:00:50.080 --> 01:00:50.720
the Ivy Leagues,

01:00:50.720 --> 01:00:51.940
the prestigious medical boards,

01:00:52.260 --> 01:00:52.720
the elite guilds,

01:00:52.720 --> 01:00:54.260
currently hold

01:00:54.260 --> 01:00:55.100
an absolute,

01:00:55.660 --> 01:00:56.980
highly lucrative monopoly

01:00:56.980 --> 01:00:57.860
on generating

01:00:57.860 --> 01:00:58.940
these safe proxies.

01:00:59.160 --> 01:00:59.920
They are the ultimate

01:00:59.920 --> 01:01:01.280
gatekeepers of opportunity.

01:01:01.720 --> 01:01:02.820
But what happens if,

01:01:03.300 --> 01:01:04.660
or more likely when,

01:01:05.060 --> 01:01:06.060
artificial intelligence

01:01:06.060 --> 01:01:06.780
reaches a point

01:01:06.780 --> 01:01:08.180
where it can directly,

01:01:08.720 --> 01:01:09.060
safely,

01:01:09.220 --> 01:01:10.400
and instantly measure

01:01:10.400 --> 01:01:11.880
a person's raw

01:01:11.880 --> 01:01:13.180
underlying competence.

01:01:13.620 --> 01:01:14.920
That is a fascinating,

01:01:15.640 --> 01:01:17.700
potentially world-breaking scenario.

01:01:18.000 --> 01:01:19.880
Imagine a highly complex,

01:01:19.880 --> 01:01:21.740
perfect digital simulation,

01:01:22.240 --> 01:01:24.380
a 15-minute AI-driven test

01:01:24.380 --> 01:01:25.380
that can determine

01:01:25.380 --> 01:01:27.500
with absolute mathematical certainty

01:01:27.500 --> 01:01:28.820
if you are a brilliant

01:01:28.820 --> 01:01:29.900
strategic thinker,

01:01:30.080 --> 01:01:31.440
or a capable coder,

01:01:31.700 --> 01:01:33.480
or even a naturally gifted surgeon,

01:01:33.940 --> 01:01:35.520
completely bypassing the need

01:01:35.520 --> 01:01:37.180
for a four-year university degree

01:01:37.180 --> 01:01:39.440
or a 10-year track record entirely.

01:01:39.440 --> 01:01:40.980
The proxy is suddenly

01:01:40.980 --> 01:01:41.900
no longer needed

01:01:41.900 --> 01:01:43.360
because the fundamental problem

01:01:43.360 --> 01:01:44.880
of opacity has been cured

01:01:44.880 --> 01:01:45.780
by technology.

01:01:46.140 --> 01:01:46.960
What happens then?

01:01:47.160 --> 01:01:47.740
Will the people

01:01:47.740 --> 01:01:48.780
currently holding power,

01:01:48.900 --> 01:01:49.640
the people who went

01:01:49.640 --> 01:01:50.560
to those elite schools

01:01:50.560 --> 01:01:51.800
and paid hundreds of thousands

01:01:51.800 --> 01:01:52.340
of dollars

01:01:52.340 --> 01:01:53.420
for their premium stickers,

01:01:53.920 --> 01:01:55.100
embrace this true,

01:01:55.300 --> 01:01:56.520
direct measurement of skill?

01:01:56.680 --> 01:01:58.120
Or will they fight tooth and nail

01:01:58.120 --> 01:01:59.060
to regulate it,

01:01:59.220 --> 01:01:59.800
discredit it,

01:01:59.840 --> 01:02:00.360
and outlaw it,

01:02:00.500 --> 01:02:01.620
desperately trying to protect

01:02:01.620 --> 01:02:02.620
the monopoly power

01:02:02.620 --> 01:02:03.460
of the expensive,

01:02:03.680 --> 01:02:04.620
wealth-gated proxies

01:02:04.620 --> 01:02:05.140
they inherited?

01:02:05.140 --> 01:02:06.440
It is the ultimate

01:02:06.440 --> 01:02:07.540
impending test

01:02:07.540 --> 01:02:08.560
of our society's

01:02:08.560 --> 01:02:09.300
stated values.

01:02:10.400 --> 01:02:11.000
Will we choose

01:02:11.000 --> 01:02:12.060
efficiency and truth,

01:02:12.440 --> 01:02:13.460
or will we protect

01:02:13.460 --> 01:02:14.060
the hierarchy?

01:02:14.680 --> 01:02:15.480
It really is.

01:02:15.580 --> 01:02:16.520
It leaves you wondering,

01:02:16.520 --> 01:02:19.020
do we really truly want

01:02:19.020 --> 01:02:20.640
a pure meritocracy

01:02:20.640 --> 01:02:22.240
where anyone from anywhere

01:02:22.240 --> 01:02:23.480
can instantly prove

01:02:23.480 --> 01:02:23.960
their worth?

01:02:24.260 --> 01:02:25.220
Or do we just want

01:02:25.220 --> 01:02:27.020
our own expensive proxies

01:02:27.020 --> 01:02:27.840
to keep winning?

01:02:28.320 --> 01:02:28.860
We want to thank you

01:02:28.860 --> 01:02:29.480
for joining us

01:02:29.480 --> 01:02:30.280
on this deep dive.

01:02:30.480 --> 01:02:31.540
We hope it encourages you

01:02:31.540 --> 01:02:32.840
to view your own credentials,

01:02:33.380 --> 01:02:34.220
the grueling path

01:02:34.220 --> 01:02:35.040
you took to get them,

01:02:35.220 --> 01:02:35.880
and the credentials

01:02:35.880 --> 01:02:36.900
of those around you

01:02:36.900 --> 01:02:38.620
in a completely new,

01:02:38.780 --> 01:02:40.020
much more structural light.

01:02:40.260 --> 01:02:41.620
The magical x-ray machine

01:02:41.620 --> 01:02:42.560
of true competence

01:02:42.560 --> 01:02:44.060
might not exist quite yet,

01:02:44.140 --> 01:02:45.520
but the certified pre-owned

01:02:45.520 --> 01:02:46.340
stickers on the window

01:02:46.340 --> 01:02:47.860
are absolutely everywhere

01:02:47.860 --> 01:02:48.940
dictating our lives

01:02:48.940 --> 01:02:49.660
if you just know

01:02:49.660 --> 01:02:50.260
how to look for them.

